- in_text_citation: [(Tang et al., unknown_d)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8c37eeef1a68434fcea64a974e15d509f9db014a)
- venue: arXiv.org
- citation count: 4
[method]
The paper evaluates a graph neural network called DCRNN for EEG seizure detection and classification, using distance-based and correlation-based graphs over 19 EEG channels. Raw EEG is transformed into log-amplitude fast Fourier transform frequency-domain inputs, then segmented into non-overlapping 12-second or 60-second clips for detection and self-supervised pre-training, and into one clip per seizure event for classification. The model is trained in a patient-disjoint setup on the TUSZ v1.5.2 dataset, using a 90/10 patient-wise split of the official training set for training and validation, while the official test set is held out after removing five patients who appear in both the official train and test sets. The component most relevant to the proposed ST-IRM method is the self-supervised pre-training of DCRNN, where pre-trained weights are used for both seizure detection and seizure classification. This relates to the proposed method's self-supervised learning component and its goal of improving cross-patient generalization, although the paper does not use invariant risk minimization, mask-based invariant/variant decomposition, patient-environment clustering, or gradient variance penalties.
[results]
Without self-supervised pre-training, distance-graph DCRNN and correlation-graph DCRNN perform on par with or better than Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines on seizure detection and classification, and outperform reported CNN results on seizure classification. With self-supervised pre-training, DCRNN improves on both tasks. For 60-second seizure detection, pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN reaches an AUROC of 0.875, matching the performance of a supervised CNN pre-trained on a labeled dataset five times larger than TUSZ. For 12-second seizure detection at a 25% false positive rate, pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN achieves 84.3% true positive rate and pre-trained correlation-graph DCRNN achieves 81.7%, compared with 72.8% for Dense-CNN, 67.0% for LSTM, and 62.5% for CNN-LSTM. For 12-second seizure classification, non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN achieves 93% accuracy on rare absence seizures, which is 2 points above the best baseline. Pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN achieves 74% accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, which is 47 points above Dense-CNN and 48 points above non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN. A neurologist review of 32 misclassified generalized non-specific test seizures found that 27 were likely focal seizures and only 5 were true generalized seizures, suggesting that label noise contributes to apparent misclassification.
[context]
Experiments use TUSZ v1.5.2 with 5,612 EEGs, 3,050 annotated seizures, eight original seizure types, and 19 standard 10-20 channels. For classification, the paper uses a refined four-class scheme: combined focal, generalized non-specific, absence, and combined tonic. Myoclonic seizures are excluded because only three examples are available, and tonic-clonic seizures are merged with tonic seizures because tonic-clonic seizures begin with a tonic phase and the tonic and tonic-clonic classes are rare. Detection and self-supervised pre-training use both seizure and non-seizure EEGs, while classification uses only seizure EEGs with one clip per seizure event. Evaluation metrics are AUROC for detection and weighted F1-score for classification, with additional ROC curves and confusion matrices. The provided text does not specify hardware, exact optimizer, learning rate, batch size, or full hyperparameter values, but the experimental conditions include patient-disjoint splits, frequency-domain log-FFT inputs, 12-second and 60-second clip lengths, and comparison against Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines trained on the same preprocessed data.
