- in_text_citation: [(Chen et al., 2020-02)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7af72a461ed7cda180e7eab878efd5f35d79bbf4)
- venue: International Conference on Machine Learning
- citation count: 27406
[method]
The paper studies a contrastive self-supervised representation-learning framework for visual data. For each input image, two correlated views are generated by sampling stochastic augmentations, including random crop and resize, color distortion, and Gaussian blur. A ResNet encoder maps each view to a representation, and a one-hidden-layer MLP projection head with a ReLU nonlinearity maps that representation to the space where the contrastive loss is computed. The loss is a normalized temperature-scaled cross-entropy objective over all 2N augmented examples in a minibatch, where the other augmented examples in the batch serve as negatives. After training, the projection head is discarded and the encoder is evaluated with linear probing and fine-tuning. This method is relevant to the proposed ST-IRM framework because ST-IRM also uses a self-supervised loss to shape representations before supervised seizure-type prediction; the paper’s design choices are directly analogous to deciding how the self-supervised loss should operate on invariant EEG representations across time steps, whether a separate projection head should be used, and how augmentation or masking should remove spurious patient-specific cues while preserving seizure-relevant structure.
[results]
In the augmentation ablation, no single transformation alone produced strong linear-evaluation performance, even though the model could identify positive pairs in the contrastive task. Composing random crop with color distortion produced the strongest tested pair scores, with the two crop-color entries in the reported ablation matrix at about 55.8 and 56.3 linear-evaluation score, while many other single transformations or pairs were lower, with several below 40. Stronger color augmentation improved linear evaluation of the unsupervised models, but the same stronger color augmentation did not improve and could hurt supervised models trained with the same augmentations. A sophisticated supervised augmentation policy did not outperform simple cropping plus stronger color distortion. The paper also reports that a nonlinear projection head improves the quality of the preceding encoder representation, and that larger batch sizes and longer training benefit contrastive learning. These results are relevant to ST-IRM because they show that self-supervised objectives learn useful representations only when the auxiliary task is made sufficiently hard by removing spurious shortcuts; for cross-patient EEG, this supports careful ablation of the mask function, patient-group environments, and time-step self-supervision to ensure the model preserves invariant seizure-type signals rather than patient-specific artifacts. The provided excerpt does not report statistical significance values for these comparisons.
[context]
The experiments were conducted on visual image data using ResNet encoders. Unsupervised contrastive models were trained for 100 or 1000 epochs and compared with supervised ResNet baselines trained for 90 epochs. Evaluation used linear probing and fine-tuning of the learned encoder. The augmentation study used an asymmetric setting in which one branch received crop and resize plus the tested transformation while the other branch was left as the identity transformation, in order to isolate the effect of individual augmentations and their compositions. Exact batch sizes, temperature values, and hardware specifications are not specified in the provided excerpt. The domain is natural images, not EEG, so the findings are methodological evidence for self-supervised representation learning rather than direct validation of cross-patient seizure classification on the TUSZ dataset.
[end related work 2]

