The paper uses a self-supervised signal-transformation recognition network to learn ECG representations for downstream emotion recognition. ECG signals from four public datasets are downsampled to 256 Hz, high-pass filtered at 0.8 Hz, normalized with user-specific z-score normalization, and segmented into non-overlapping 10-second windows. Each segment is transformed using six pretext operations: noise addition, scaling, permutation, time-warping, temporal inversion, and negation. A multi-task convolutional network trained in TensorFlow predicts which transformation was applied, with the total loss computed as a weighted average of the six transformation-classification losses. The learned encoder is then used for emotion recognition. This relates to the proposed spatiotemporal invariant risk minimization plan because it is a self-supervised representation-learning strategy for physiological time series and examines whether multi-task and multi-dataset self-supervision improves downstream generalization, but it does not use invariant risk minimization, patient-level environments, or an explicit invariant/variant mask.
[results]
The provided excerpt does not report numerical accuracy, F1-score, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance. It reports qualitatively that multi-task self-supervision consistently outperforms single-task self-supervision, with stronger gains on AMIGOS and DREAMER. It also reports that training the self-supervised network on all four datasets combined improves emotion recognition compared with training on individual datasets, with significant improvements for AMIGOS, DREAMER, and WESAD and only marginal improvement for SWELL. The identified optimal parameter vector is SNR 15, scaling factor 0.9, 20 permutation segments, 9 time-warping segments, and stretch factor 1.05. The loss weights are 0.0125 for temporal inversion and negation, and 0.195 for the other five transformation tasks.
