[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Krueger et al., 2020-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3621fff4a1c791901ea4a1359c10575193ec712d)
- venue: International Conference on Machine Learning
- citation count: 1330
[method]
The paper evaluates Risk Extrapolation (REx) for domain generalization, with two variants: MM-REx and V-REx. MM-REx minimizes average training risk plus a robust term over affine combinations of domain risks with coefficients bounded by a hyperparameter lambda_min; negative lambda_min extrapolates beyond the convex hull of domain risks and, as lambda_min approaches negative infinity, enforces equality of domain risks, while lambda_min=0 recovers risk interpolation. V-REx minimizes average risk plus beta times the variance of domain risks, where beta=0 recovers empirical risk minimization and large beta enforces equal risks. The paper also argues that exact risk equalization can support causal discovery under assumptions that the causes of the label are observed. The experimental setting described in detail is Colored MNIST, a binary classification task in which digits 0-4 and 5-9 are collapsed into two classes, color is a spurious feature strongly correlated with the label in training, and the color-label correlation is reversed at test time. The model must learn digit shape and ignore color. The paper compares V-REx, MM-REx, IRM, risk interpolation, empirical risk minimization, and a grayscale oracle using test accuracy. The paper also evaluates on DomainBed and reinforcement learning tasks, but the provided excerpt gives detailed quantitative results for Colored MNIST.
[results]
On Colored MNIST, V-REx achieved 71.5 ± 1.0 training accuracy and 68.7 ± 0.9 test accuracy. IRM achieved 70.8 ± 0.9 training accuracy and 66.9 ± 2.5 test accuracy. MM-REx achieved 72.4 ± 1.8 training accuracy and 66.1 ± 1.5 test accuracy. Risk interpolation achieved 88.9 ± 0.3 training accuracy and 22.3 ± 4.6 test accuracy. Empirical risk minimization achieved 87.4 ± 0.2 training accuracy and 17.1 ± 0.6 test accuracy. A grayscale oracle achieved 73.5 ± 0.2 training accuracy and 73.0 ± 0.4 test accuracy, with an optimum of 75 and chance of 50. V-REx outperformed IRM by 1.8 test accuracy points and MM-REx by 2.6 test accuracy points, but remained 4.3 points below the grayscale oracle. The paper also reports that REx outperforms IRM on a wide range of out-of-distribution tasks, but when inherent noise in the label varies across environments, IRM succeeds and REx performs poorly.
[context]
The Colored MNIST experiment uses three training domains, a binary digit classification task, and a test distribution in which the spurious color-label association is reversed; evaluation is by accuracy. The provided excerpt does not specify hardware, optimizer, batch size, or exact hyperparameter values for these experiments. This paper is relevant to the proposed ST-IRM research plan because both use multiple environments or domains to encourage invariance: the proposed method partitions patients into groups and applies invariant risk minimization with a gradient variance penalty, while the paper uses V-REx to penalize variance of domain risks and MM-REx to robustly extrapolate across domain risks. The paper's evidence that equalizing risks can suppress spurious features supports using patient groups as environments to remove patient-specific EEG variation, but its warning that REx can fail when label noise varies across environments is directly relevant to EEG, where artifacts, noise, and patient-specific electrophysiological variation may differ across patient groups. The proposed mask and self-supervised temporal loss are not present in the paper, but the paper's risk-equality principle provides a related inductive bias for separating invariant seizure-related signals from variant patient-specific signals.
[end related work 4]

