[method]
The paper uses SimSiam, a self-supervised siamese representation-learning method. It takes two augmented views of an image, processes both with a shared encoder consisting of a ResNet-50 backbone and a three-layer projection MLP, and uses a two-layer prediction MLP on one branch to predict the other branch's normalized encoder output. The loss is the symmetrized negative cosine similarity between the predicted vector and the stop-gradient-applied target vector, equivalent to mean squared error on L2-normalized vectors up to a scale factor. Stop-gradient is applied to the target in each term so that one branch's encoder does not receive gradient from its own target in that term. This is relevant to the proposed ST-IRM plan because the plan also uses a self-supervised loss to align invariant representations across time steps; SimSiam provides a collapse-avoiding self-supervised alignment objective that could be adapted to EEG time-step or augmented-view pairs, although the paper does not include invariant masking, environment partitioning, invariant risk minimization, or cross-patient EEG evaluation.
[results]
On ImageNet linear evaluation, SimSiam was trained with batch size 256 and used neither negative samples nor a momentum encoder. The excerpt reports that it achieved competitive results, had the highest accuracy among compared methods under 100-epoch pre-training, and outperformed SimCLR in all cases, though its gain from longer training was smaller. Exact SimSiam accuracy values are not included in the provided text. In transfer-learning experiments on VOC and COCO object detection and instance segmentation, SimSiam representations were competitive with leading methods after 200-epoch ImageNet pre-training; a recipe with learning rate 0.5 and weight decay 1e-5 produced better results in all tasks than the base recipe. Related ablations reported values of 66.5, 66.4, and 66.0 for SimCLR with predictor and predictor plus stop-gradient, and 66.5 for SwAV with predictor, 65.2 and NaN when stop-gradient was removed, indicating that stop-gradient behavior is method-specific.
[context]
Experiments used ImageNet with standard ResNet-50 and two 224x224 augmented views during pre-training. Default pre-training used SGD with base learning rate 0.05 scaled by batch size divided by 256, cosine decay, weight decay 0.0001, momentum 0.9, batch size 512 by default, and synchronized batch normalization; the reported SimSiam ImageNet comparison used batch size 256. The projection MLP had three layers, hidden dimension 2048, batch normalization on each fully connected layer including output, and no ReLU on output. The prediction MLP had two layers, input/output dimension 2048, hidden dimension 512, batch normalization on hidden layers, and no batch normalization or ReLU on output. Ablation pre-training used 100 epochs, while transfer-learning comparisons used 200-epoch pre-training and end-to-end fine-tuning on target datasets. The setup was described as friendly to typical 8-GPU implementations, but specific hardware for the main results is not detailed in the excerpt.
