- venue: Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition
- citation count: 13661
[method]
The paper studies a masked autoencoder for self-supervised pretraining of Vision Transformers. Input images are divided into non-overlapping patches; a high fraction of patches is removed by uniform random sampling without replacement. The encoder sees only visible patches and does not use mask tokens, while a lightweight decoder receives the encoder latent representation plus mask tokens and reconstructs pixel values for the full image. This relates to the proposed ST-IRM plan only at the level of using a self-supervised, mask-based representation-learning stage before supervised classification; it does not implement invariant risk minimization, patient-group environments, gradient variance penalties, or EEG-specific spatiotemporal modeling.
[results]
On ImageNet-1K, ViT-L/16 pretraining with the masked autoencoder and 50-epoch fine-tuning reached 84.9% top-1 accuracy, compared with 76.5% for supervised training from scratch in the original setting and 82.5% for an improved supervised-from-scratch implementation. Larger models improved further: ViT-H reached 86.9% at 224 resolution and 87.8% at 448 resolution using only ImageNet-1K, exceeding the prior ImageNet-1K-only result of 87.1%. In transfer to COCO with Mask R-CNN, the method improved box AP over supervised pretraining by 2.4 points for ViT-B (50.3 versus 47.9) and by 4.0 points for ViT-L (53.3 versus 49.3). It was also reported as more accurate than token-based BEiT while being faster, and better than MoCo v3 when one or more Transformer blocks were fine-tuned.
[context]
Experiments used ImageNet-1K for self-supervised pretraining and evaluation with a single 224x224 validation crop. The main comparison used ViT-L/16, with high-ratio random patch masking, an asymmetric encoder-decoder design, and fine-tuning for 50 epochs versus 200 epochs for supervised training from scratch. For the stronger reported models, pretraining was run for 1600 epochs; ViT-L training on 128 TPU-v3 cores took 31 hours for 1600 epochs, compared with 36 hours for 300 epochs for MoCo v3. Downstream COCO evaluation adapted the ViT backbone to Mask R-CNN with FPN. The paper's setting is natural-image classification and transfer, not cross-patient EEG seizure classification, and it does not evaluate distribution shift or invariant risk minimization.
