[method]
The paper's closest related method is a multi-task self-supervised signal transformation recognition network for ECG emotion recognition. It learns ECG representations by training a CNN to distinguish original 10-second ECG segments from six generated transformations: noise addition, scaling, permutation, temporal inversion, negation, and time-warping. The temporal transformations, especially permutation, temporal inversion, and time-warping, are related to the proposed method because they encourage the model to capture temporal structure and invariances in physiological time series, similar to the proposed temporal consistency loss between invariant representations at adjacent time steps. The multi-task self-supervision and multi-dataset training are also related to the research plan's goal of learning robust spatiotemporal representations across heterogeneous subjects, although the paper does not use an invariant mask, invariant risk minimization, gradient variance penalty, or cross-patient EEG seizure classification.
[results]
The paper reports that multi-task self-supervised representation learning consistently outperformed single-task self-supervision, with more prominent gains on AMIGOS and DREAMER. The optimal multi-task parameter vector was SNR 15, scaling factor 0.9, 20 permutation segments, 9 time-warping segments, and stretch factor 1.05. Single-task analysis showed that emotion recognition F1 was best when pretext tasks had moderate difficulty; performance dropped when transformations were too easy, such as SNR greater than 35, scaling factor between 0.95 and 1.05, or 2 permutation segments, and also when transformations were too distorted, such as SNR less than 10, more than 20 permutation segments, scaling factor less than 0.6 or greater than 1.4, or stretch factor greater than 1.4. Training all four datasets together improved emotion recognition over training on individual datasets, with significant improvements for AMIGOS, DREAMER, and WESAD and marginal improvement for SWELL. Exact F1 values are not provided in the supplied text.
[context]
Experiments used four ECG datasets: AMIGOS, DREAMER, WESAD, and SWELL. Signals were downsampled to 256 Hz where needed, high-pass filtered at 0.8 Hz, user-specific z-score normalized, and segmented into non-overlapping 10-second windows. Evaluation used 10-fold cross-validation with 90 percent training and 10 percent testing repeated 10 times after shuffling; parameter analyses used a single 90-10 split. The transformation recognition network was trained for 100 epochs and the emotion recognition network for 250 epochs using Adam with learning rate 0.001 and batch size 128 in TensorFlow. Loss weights were 0.0125 for temporal inversion and negation and 0.195 for the other five transformation losses. Temporal inversion and negation losses saturated after 5 to 7 epochs, which motivated their smaller loss weights. The paper evaluates emotion recognition F1 accuracy, not cross-patient seizure classification metrics, and does not report hardware details in the provided excerpt.
