[method]
The paper evaluates SimSiam, a non-contrastive self-supervised learning method that processes two augmented views of an image with a shared encoder f and a prediction MLP h. The encoder f consists of a ResNet-50 backbone plus a 3-layer projection MLP with batch normalization on each fully connected layer, hidden dimension 2048, and an output layer without ReLU. The prediction MLP h has 2 layers, input and output dimension 2048, hidden dimension 512, batch normalization on the hidden layer, and an output layer without batch normalization or ReLU. The loss is a symmetrized negative cosine similarity between the predicted representation of one view and the stop-gradient target representation of the other view, equivalent to mean squared error between L2-normalized vectors up to a scale of 2. This method is related to the proposed EEG research method because the proposed Lssl also aligns a transformed previous-time invariant representation, z_{t-1}(m(phi(X_{i,t-1}))), with a current-time invariant representation, m(phi(X_{i,t})). SimSiam provides a relevant template for using a predictor and stop-gradient to preserve relationships between two representations without negative samples or a momentum encoder, which could be adapted from image augmentations to adjacent EEG time steps and invariant mask outputs.
[results]
On ImageNet linear evaluation with a standard ResNet-50 and two 224x224 views, SimSiam was trained with batch size 256, no negative samples, and no momentum encoder. The paper reports that SimSiam achieves competitive results, has the highest accuracy among compared methods under 100-epoch pre-training, and outperforms SimCLR in all reported cases, although its gain from longer training is smaller. The provided excerpt does not state the exact SimSiam ImageNet linear evaluation accuracy. In transfer learning to VOC and COCO object detection and instance segmentation after 200-epoch ImageNet pre-training, SimSiam is competitive with leading methods, and a recipe with learning rate 0.5 and weight decay 1e-5 gives better transfer results than the base recipe while having similar ImageNet accuracy. Specific ablation values reported for related methods are: SimCLR with predictor 66.5, SimCLR with predictor and stop-gradient 66.4 and 66.0; SwAV with predictor 66.5, SwAV with stop-gradient removed 65.2 and then NaN. These outcomes are relevant to the proposed research plan because they show that a predictor and stop-gradient can support self-supervised representation learning, but the necessity of stop-gradient depends on the objective: it was not helpful for SimCLR with negatives, while removing it from SwAV caused degradation and divergence. This informs the design of the proposed temporal invariant Lssl, especially if it is used as a non-contrastive objective for EEG invariant representations.
[context]
Experimental conditions include ImageNet pre-training with SGD, base learning rate 0.05 scaled by batch size divided by 256, cosine decay, weight decay 0.0001, momentum 0.9, default batch size 512 for ablations but batch size 256 for the main ImageNet comparison, synchronized batch normalization, 100-epoch pre-training for ablations, and 200-epoch pre-training for transfer comparisons. The method is described as friendly to typical 8-GPU implementations. Evaluation uses ImageNet linear probing, kNN validation accuracy as a progress monitor, and end-to-end fine-tuning on VOC and COCO detection and instance segmentation with per-method fine-tuning learning-rate search. This context matters for the proposed EEG study because the paper demonstrates that a simple self-supervised alignment objective can be trained without large-batch LARS or negative sampling, but the image-domain results do not directly validate temporal EEG invariance or cross-patient seizure classification. For the proposed TUSZ-based plan, analogous controls would be needed for temporal sequence length, invariant mask dimensionality, batch size of 40 EEG clips, collapse monitoring, and whether stop-gradient is applied to the current-time invariant target in Lssl.
