[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6)
- venue: International Conference on Learning Representations
- citation count: 175
[method]
The paper uses a graph-based DCRNN for EEG seizure detection and classification on the TUSZ dataset. Raw EEG signals from 19 standard 10-20 channels are transformed into log-amplitude fast Fourier transform frequency-domain inputs. The EEG channels are represented as either a distance graph or a correlation graph, and a DCRNN processes the graph-structured EEG clips. A self-supervised pre-training stage is applied to seizure and non-seizure 12-second and 60-second clips, after which the pretrained DCRNN weights are used to initialize supervised models for downstream seizure detection and classification. This method is related to the proposed research method because both use self-supervised learning on sequential TUSZ EEG clips to improve seizure classification, but the paper’s approach is a clip-level self-supervised pre-training baseline rather than an explicit step-level loss that preserves invariant representations across time steps. It also does not use an invariant mask, patient-environment partitioning, invariant risk minimization, or a gradient variance penalty as described in the proposed plan.
[results]
Without self-supervised pre-training, distance-graph DCRNN and correlation-graph DCRNN performed on par with or better than Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines. With self-supervised pre-training, distance-graph DCRNN achieved an AUROC of 0.875 for 60-second seizure detection, comparable to a CNN pretrained with supervised labels on a dataset about five times larger. At a 25% false positive rate on 12-second clips, pretrained distance-graph DCRNN reached 84.3% true positive rate and pretrained correlation-graph DCRNN reached 81.7%, compared with 72.8% for Dense-CNN, 67.0% for LSTM, and 62.5% for CNN-LSTM. For 12-second seizure classification, non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN achieved 93% accuracy on rare absence seizures, 2 points above the best baseline. Pretrained distance-graph DCRNN achieved 74% accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, 47 points above Dense-CNN and 48 points above non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN. The same pretrained weights were used for both detection and classification, indicating task-general initialization. The paper also reports that frequency-domain inputs substantially outperformed time-domain inputs, although exact values are not provided in the included sections. A neurologist reviewed 32 misclassified generalized non-specific test seizures and found that 27 were likely focal seizures, suggesting label noise contributed to apparent misclassification. No statistical significance tests are reported.
[context]
Experiments used TUSZ v1.5.2, containing 5,612 EEGs and 3,050 annotated seizures across eight seizure types. The authors used 19 EEG channels, log-amplitude FFT preprocessing, and non-overlapping 12-second or 60-second clips for seizure detection and self-supervised pre-training. For seizure classification, one 12-second or 60-second clip was taken from each seizure event. The seizure taxonomy was reduced to four classes: combined focal, generalized non-specific, absence, and combined tonic. Data were split by patient, with a 90/10 train/validation split from the official training set and the official test set held out after removing five patients who appeared in both the official train and test sets. Evaluation used AUROC for seizure detection and weighted F1-score for seizure classification. Baselines were trained and evaluated on the same preprocessed data. Hardware, the exact self-supervised objective, and full hyperparameters are not specified in the provided sections. Relative to the proposed research plan, this paper provides a relevant self-supervised pre-training baseline on the same TUSZ frequency-domain EEG setup, but it focuses on general graph GNN initialization rather than explicit invariant spatiotemporal representation learning across patient environments.
[end related work 7]

