[method]
The paper implements a masked autoencoder for self-supervised visual representation learning. Images are divided into non-overlapping patches, a high fraction of patches is randomly sampled without replacement and removed, and the encoder processes only the visible patches without mask tokens. A lightweight decoder then reconstructs the full pixel image from the encoder latent representation and mask tokens. The method is evaluated by pretraining on ImageNet-1K and then assessing representations through linear probing, end-to-end fine-tuning of Vision Transformer backbones, and transfer learning to object detection and segmentation with Mask R-CNN and FPN. This is related to the proposed EEG method in that both use masking and self-supervised pretraining to learn representations before supervised classification, but the paper masks image patches for pixel reconstruction rather than applying a learned mask to EEG features to isolate invariant components, and it does not include a temporal consistency loss, patient grouping, or invariant risk minimization across patient environments.
[results]
For ViT-L/16 on ImageNet-1K, supervised training from scratch achieved 76.5 percent top-1 accuracy in the original recipe and 82.5 percent with the paper stronger regularization recipe, while MAE pretraining followed by 50-epoch fine-tuning reached 84.9 percent. Larger models improved further: ViT-H reached 86.9 percent at 224x224 and 87.8 percent at 448x448, exceeding the reported previous best of 87.1 percent at 512x512 using only ImageNet-1K. In COCO transfer, MAE pretraining improved Mask R-CNN box AP over supervised pretraining by 2.4 points for ViT-B, from 47.9 to 50.3, and by 4.0 points for ViT-L, from 49.3 to 53.3. The method was also reported to be faster than token-based BEiT by about 3.5 times per epoch and to require less total pretraining time than some contrastive baselines when compared on the same hardware. The provided excerpt does not report confidence intervals or statistical significance tests.
[context]
Experiments used ImageNet-1K training data with no external data, single 224x224 crop validation, random patch masking with a high masking ratio, no data augmentation, and an asymmetric encoder-decoder design. The main ablation used ViT-L/16, and stronger models used ViT-H. Pretraining for the main comparisons used 1600 epochs; on 128 TPU-v3 cores, ViT-L MAE pretraining took 31 hours for 1600 epochs, compared with 36 hours for 300 epochs for a contrastive baseline. Fine-tuning used 50 epochs rather than 200 epochs from scratch. Transfer learning used Mask R-CNN with FPN on COCO and reported box AP and mask AP. These conditions are relevant to the proposed EEG research plan as a benchmark for self-supervised pretraining followed by supervised fine-tuning, but they do not include EEG signals, temporal sequences, cross-patient disjoint splits, seizure-type labels, or invariant risk minimization across patient environments.
