[method]
The paper used a reconstruction-based self-supervised pretraining approach for EEG, described as BENDR/MAEEG, which is related to the proposed method because both use masked EEG features and self-supervised objectives to learn temporally structured representations for downstream classification. Raw 6-channel 100 Hz EEG was passed through six convolutional layers with dropout, GroupNorm, and GELU to produce 64-dimensional temporal features at approximately 1.05 Hz. A mask selected a subset of feature tokens and replaced them with Gaussian noise N(0, 1/64). The masked sequence was encoded by an 8-layer transformer encoder with convolutional relative positional embeddings to 192-dimensional features, then mapped by a final convolutional layer to 64-dimensional contextual features. A contrastive loss compared contextual features with the corresponding unmasked features. The paper then varied mask rate and number of mask chunks to study how masking affects learned representations and downstream sleep-stage classification. Unlike the proposed invariant spatiotemporal framework, this method did not decompose features into invariant and variant components, did not use a learned mask m(·) in [0,1], did not apply invariant risk minimization, and did not target cross-patient seizure-type classification.
[results]
The paper reported that higher masking percentages with fewer chunks, producing longer masked spans, increased the SSL pretraining loss, indicating a harder reconstruction/contrastive task. Downstream performance differences were largest when only 0.1% of downstream labels were available, corresponding to about 4 hours of labeled data. Under this low-label condition, masking 75% of tokens performed better overall than masking 50% or 25%, and a single mask chunk outperformed multiple chunks. All MAEEG-pretrained models outperformed models without pretraining. The same trend—longer single mask spans performing better than shorter spans under limited labels—was reported for both MAEEG and BENDR. The excerpt did not provide exact classification scores, confidence intervals, or statistical significance values, so the outcomes are relative comparisons rather than quantitative absolute performance.
[context]
Experiments used the 2018 PhysioNet Challenge sleep EEG dataset, containing overnight polysomnography from 994 subjects, about 7,000 hours, 65% male, mean age 55 years ± 14.4, split into 596 training, 198 validation, and 200 test subjects. Each polysomnogram included 6-channel EEG, ECG, and respiratory signals, with labels for five sleep stages (wake, N1, N2, N3, REM) and respiratory-effort-related arousals assigned in 30-second non-overlapping windows. The paper focused on 6-channel EEG for five-stage sleep classification. Models were implemented in PyTorch 1.8.1; hardware specifications were not provided. This context is relevant to the proposed research plan because it demonstrates that masked self-supervised pretraining on EEG can improve low-label downstream classification, but it differs from the proposed plan in task (sleep stages rather than cross-patient seizure types), channel count (6 rather than 19), temporal segmentation (30-second windows rather than 60-second clips segmented into 1-second intervals), and absence of invariant/variant masking, environment partitioning, and gradient variance penalties.
