[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Mirowski et al., 2009-11)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f94ed69179beb00b7d759b75f37e3b7f8d0fc937)
- venue: Clinical Neurophysiology
- citation count: 411
[method]
The paper used a patient-specific supervised seizure-prediction pipeline on invasive EEG. It computed bivariate synchronization features, including cross-correlation, nonlinear interdependence, difference of short-term Lyapunov exponents, phase-locking synchrony, phase-difference entropy, and wavelet coherence, from six intracranial channels. These features were aggregated into spatio-temporal or spatio-temporal-frequency patterns and classified as preictal or interictal using logistic regression, convolutional networks, or support vector machines. This is related to the proposed supervised loss Lsup = (1/|n|) sum L(hT(m(phi(X_iT))), yi) because both use a supervised head to make a representation predictive of a seizure-related label. In the paper, the classifier corresponds to the supervised head applied to a handcrafted spatiotemporal feature representation, whereas the proposed method applies the supervised head to a masked invariant representation. The paper also used L1-norm regularization in logistic regression and convolutional networks to drive unused weights toward zero and then performed sensitivity analysis to identify discriminative channel pairs and frequency bands. This is conceptually related to the proposed mask function that selects invariant components, but the paper's selection is post-hoc and patient-specific rather than an explicit invariant mask trained across patient environments.
[results]
The best combination, wavelet coherence patterns classified by convolutional networks, predicted all test seizures with zero false positives in 15 of 21 patients, corresponding to 71% sensitivity. Phase-locking synchrony with convolutional networks achieved perfect prediction in 13 of 21 patients, or 62% sensitivity. Wavelet coherence with logistic regression and phase-difference entropy with convolutional networks each achieved perfect prediction in 11 of 21 patients, or 52% sensitivity. Phase-locking synchrony with logistic regression and nonlinear interdependence with convolutional networks each achieved perfect prediction in 10 of 21 patients, or 48% sensitivity. Across feature types, convolutional networks achieved zero-false-alarm prediction in 20 of 21 patients, logistic regression in 14 of 21 patients, and support vector machines in 11 of 21 patients. At least one feature-classifier combination predicted all test seizures for every patient, on average about 60 minutes before onset, with no false alarms. Training samples were classified with 100% accuracy for most patients, except patients 17, 19, and 21, where the penalty was adjusted to allow false negatives but no false positives. These results outperformed previously reported performance on the same dataset of 42% sensitivity and 3 false positives per day. Feature selection showed that, for non-frequency features, only 3 or 4 of 15 channel pairs were usually necessary, and for wavelet-based features only a subset of frequency bands was discriminative, always including high-frequency synchronization features.
[context]
The experiments were conducted on the Freiburg invasive EEG database from 21 patients with medically intractable focal epilepsy. Each patient had 2 to 6 seizures, at least 50 minutes of preictal data for most seizures, and about 24 hours of interictal EEG spanning wake-sleep. The dataset was restricted to six channels: three focal electrodes involved in early ictal activity and three electrodes not involved in seizure spread. EEG was sampled at 256 Hz and digitized to 16 bits. Preprocessing used IIR elliptical filters: a 49-51 Hz band-reject 12th-order filter, a 120 Hz first-order low-pass filter, and a 0.5 Hz fifth-order high-pass filter. Data were scaled per patient to zero mean and unit variance for logistic regression and convolutional networks, or to the range -1 to 1 for support vector machines. Training and testing were patient-specific: preictal samples from the last one or two seizures and 33% of interictal samples were held out as test data, while earlier seizures and remaining interictal data were used for training; 10% or 20% of training data were used for cross-validation. The task was binary preictal versus interictal prediction, not cross-patient seizure-type classification, and no environment partitioning, self-supervised temporal loss, or invariant risk minimization was used. Computational hardware and detailed hyperparameters were not reported in the provided text.
[end related work 1]

