- citation count: 39
[method]
The paper used a supervised handcrafted-feature EEG classification pipeline that is related to the proposed supervised loss because it trains a labeled classifier to predict epileptiform events from EEG representations, but it does not use an invariant mask, self-supervised temporal objective, or invariant risk minimization. Raw 21-channel scalp EEG was recorded at 200 Hz with 12-bit resolution in unipolar and bipolar montages, segmented into 2-second epochs, and labeled by two experts as normal, spike, or seizure. After artifact rejection for amplitudes above 100 mV, 0.1-70 Hz band-pass filtering, 60 Hz notch filtering, and Daubechies 4 wavelet filtering, 1700 features per epoch were extracted. A genetic algorithm selected features and tuned an SVM classifier for two-class problems, and a post-classification spike-matching module used bipolar derivatives and phase-reversal logic to refine spike localization. The setup is relevant to the research plan as a supervised baseline for seizure-related EEG detection and a limited cross-dataset generalization test, but it targets spike/seizure detection rather than multi-class cross-patient seizure-type classification and does not explicitly separate invariant from patient-specific representations.
[results]
For Data Set I, the GA-SVM classifier achieved 83.02% accuracy for spike detection and 99.77% for seizure detection, compared with 65.59% and 95.05% for SVM without GA. The best classifier used SampEn and total variation-type features, and the top five feature types represented nearly 80% of the top 100 selected features. Adding post-classification spike matching increased spike accuracy from 83.02% to 86.69% but decreased normal EEG recognition by 0.51%; ROC analysis reported the largest area under the curve among tested classifiers, while SVM-only was the worst. When trained on all Data Set I and tested on the holdout Data Set II, spike detection accuracy was 91.18% and seizure detection accuracy was 99.22%, close to Data Set I values of 86.69% and 99.77%. No statistical significance tests or confidence intervals were reported.
[context]
Data Set I comprised 13 outpatients, including 5 temporal lobe epilepsy patients with abnormal EEG and 8 controls with normal EEG, and contained 1939 normal, 436 spike, and 444 seizure 2-second epochs; half of the epochs were used for training and half for prediction. Data Set II comprised 5 inpatients, including 3 with active spikes or frequent seizures and 2 with non-epileptic seizure, and contained approximately 360 hours of bedside EEG, including about 6 hours of seizures and 1 hour of spikes, and was used as a holdout test set. EEG was acquired with 21 Ag-AgCl electrodes in the 10-20 system, digitized at 200 Hz over 12 bits, and processed in MATLAB. The study evaluated accuracy, sensitivity, and specificity using confusion tables, but reported mainly accuracy. The small Data Set II patient count, class imbalance in real recordings, and focus on spike/seizure versus normal rather than seizure subtype make the generalization evidence limited relative to the proposed cross-patient, multi-class invariant representation learning plan.
