- in_text_citation: [(Albaqami et al., 2022-11)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1472efddbffc5691353a0bc3b1b2d6511f2ae36a)
- venue: Biomedical Signal Processing and Control
- citation count: 56
[method]
The paper's related method is a supervised multi-path deep classifier, named MP-SeizNet, for specific seizure-type classification using EEG from the TUSZ v1.5.2 dataset. The pipeline selects a 20-channel transverse central parietal montage, resamples recordings to 250 Hz when necessary, and crops each recording into non-overlapping 2-second segments. Non-specific seizure labels and the myoclonic seizure class are excluded, leaving a set of specific seizure types for classification. The model combines a CNN sub-model and an attention-based Bi-LSTM sub-model, and the final multi-path network is trained with a cross-entropy supervised loss using the Adam optimizer. This method is related to the proposed supervised loss Lsup because it uses seizure-type labels to train a predictor on EEG-derived features, but it does not explicitly apply the supervised loss to a masked invariant representation m(phi(X)), nor does it include invariant risk minimization, self-supervised temporal consistency, patient-environment partitioning, or gradient variance penalties. It therefore provides a supervised classification reference and a patient-wise evaluation protocol relevant to the research plan's cross-patient seizure-classification goal, rather than a direct implementation of the proposed invariant representation method.
[results]
In the patient-wise cross-validation scenario, MP-SeizNet achieved a weighted F1-score of 87.62%, outperforming the CNN and Bi-LSTM-attention sub-models by more than 1 to 3 percentage points. In the seizure-wise cross-validation scenario, MP-SeizNet achieved a weighted F1-score of 98.01%, while the CNN sub-model produced a result within less than 0.1 percentage points of this value. The attention-based Bi-LSTM sub-model achieved an F1-score of 97.04%, and the paper reports that adding the attention mechanism improved performance by more than 10 percentage points relative to a Bi-LSTM without attention. The paper also reports 95% confidence intervals from 20 randomized tests as F1 0.976 with CI 0.975-0.977 and F1 0.963 with CI 0.961-0.965. Additional confusion-matrix-derived measures, including sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, negative predictive value, and AUC, were reported, but the provided excerpt does not include all per-class numerical values.
[context]
Experiments used TUSZ v1.5.2, described as containing over 300 patients and more than 3000 seizure events, with scalp EEG recorded at 250-512 Hz using the International 10-20 system. The primary metric was weighted F1-score because of class imbalance, supplemented by confusion-matrix-derived statistical measures and 95% confidence intervals. Seizure-wise evaluation used 5-fold cross-validation with proportional class distribution in each fold and an 80:20 training-validation split; this setup allowed the same patient to appear in training, validation, and test folds. Patient-wise evaluation used 3-fold cross-validation with disjoint patient sets, motivated by the need to assess generalization to unseen patients; because some classes had limited patient coverage, the paper notes that when test data for a class was substantially larger than training data, a subset of test data was selected to cover the start, middle, and end of seizure events. Training used 100 epochs, batch size 64, early stopping with patience of 10 epochs on validation loss, cross-entropy loss, Adam optimization, initial learning rate 0.01, and learning-rate reduction with a minimum value of 1e-4. No hardware or computational resource details are provided in the extracted text. The method does not explicitly model patient-specific distribution shift through invariant representations, so its patient-wise results are relevant to the research plan's cross-patient objective but do not directly validate the proposed invariant mask or invariant risk minimization components.
