[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Zhong et al., 2023-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7389f76fc63767154fa213fc436a13b47de00e7b)
- venue: Frontiers in Neuroscience
- citation count: 14
[method]
The paper uses a supervised handcrafted-feature pipeline for binary preictal/interictal seizure prediction from EEG. Raw scalp or intracranial EEG is preprocessed, segmented into 5 s non-overlapping epochs, and converted into spatiotemporal features including fuzzy entropy, power spectral density, and spatial brain-network features. An optimal spatiotemporal feature set is selected using a score that combines independence and information criteria. The selected features are classified by a radial-basis-function support vector machine with default kernel parameters, and continuous 5 s predictions are post-processed with a 1-min majority vote. This method is related to the proposed supervised loss Lsup because it uses supervised labels to train a classifier on EEG-derived spatiotemporal representations, but it does not implement an invariant mask m(·), invariant risk minimization, cross-patient disjoint splits, or seizure-type classification; it is a subject-specific binary prediction baseline rather than the proposed invariant cross-patient representation learner.
[results]
On the Kaggle iEEG dataset, the full spatiotemporal feature set achieved average accuracy of 98.01%, AUC of 0.96, F-score of 98.30%, and false positive rate of 3.83%. Dog4 reached 100% accuracy, while Dog5 had a false positive rate of 11%. Feature-selection ablation showed that no feature selection gave the lowest accuracy, independence-based or information-based selection gave accuracy below 80%, and the proposed independence-plus-information feature score achieved the highest accuracy, 98.01% on Kaggle and 95.93% on CHB-MIT. Temporal-only features yielded average accuracy of 87.7%, AUC of 0.84, F-score of 87.91%, and false positive rate of 4.42%; spatial-only features yielded accuracy of 81.75%, AUC of 0.73, F-score of 80.91%, and false positive rate of 19.64%. On the CHB-MIT scalp EEG dataset, the full method achieved average accuracy of 95.93%, AUC of 0.92, F-score of 94.97%, and false positive rate of 0.0473/h. Several patients exceeded 99% accuracy, while chb07 had the lowest accuracy at 85.12%. Temporal-only features achieved accuracy of 86.4%, AUC of 0.81, F-score of 84.49%, and false positive rate of 7.01%; spatial-only features achieved accuracy of 82.19%, AUC of 0.77, F-score of 82.66%, and false positive rate of 11.17%. Compared with existing state-of-the-art methods, the paper reports better false positive rate on both datasets and better Kaggle performance, while CHB-MIT accuracy is slightly lower than two deep-learning baselines that did not report false positive rate.
[context]
Experiments used two public datasets. The CHB-MIT scalp EEG dataset used 14 of 23 patients, recorded at 256 Hz using the 10-20 system; preictal states were defined as the 30 min before seizure onset, interictal states were at least 4 h away from any seizure, and patients were retained only if they had at least three preictal and interictal states. The Kaggle iEEG dataset used 5 dogs, 16 electrodes, 400 Hz sampling, 44 seizures, preictal states of 1 h before onset, and interictal signals at least 1 week before or after seizures. Segmentation used 5 s non-overlapping windows. Preprocessing included mean removal, a 0.5-70 Hz fourth-order Butterworth bandpass filter, a 50 Hz notch filter for scalp EEG, and independent component analysis for artifact removal; severe artifacts were excluded. Implementation used MATLAB R2016b with EEGLAB and statistics toolbox, and no GPU or other hardware details were provided. The support vector machine used the default radial basis function kernel, and post-processing used a 1-min majority vote. Evaluation metrics were accuracy, AUC, F-score, and false positive rate, reported per subject and averaged across subjects. The setup is not a cross-patient evaluation and does not classify seizure types, so it provides only a related supervised EEG prediction baseline for the proposed invariant cross-patient seizure-type classification plan.
[end related work 4]


