[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Asif et al., 2019-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/457931ded7106a4fa26bc84844e1323cecac231f)
- venue: MLCN/RNO-AI@MICCAI
- citation count: 94
[method]
The paper used SeizureNet, an ensemble of deep CNN models trained on saliency-encoded spectrograms derived from raw EEG time-series signals. The framework applied Fast Fourier Transform-based saliency encoding and multi-spectral feature learning to capture seizure-related information across multiple frequency and spatial resolutions. Sub-network logits were combined by averaging, and the ensemble was trained with a cross-entropy loss against seizure-type labels. Experiments used the TUH EEG Seizure Corpus, specifically v1.4.0 and v1.5.2, with Myoclonic seizures excluded because only three examples were available. For v1.5.2, the paper used patient-wise 3-fold cross-validation so that training and test seizures came from different patients; for v1.4.0, it used seizure-wise 5-fold cross-validation because two seizure types contained data from only two patients. Training used the Torch library, Adam optimization, batch size 50, 400 epochs, initial learning rate 0.001 with reductions by a factor of 10 at 50% and 75% of training, parameter decay 0.0005, zero-mean Gaussian weight initialization with standard deviation 0.01, and zero-initialized biases. Evaluation used weighted F1 score as the primary metric.
[results]
SeizureNet improved patient-wise weighted F1 score by approximately 3 percentage points and seizure-wise weighted F1 score by approximately 4 percentage points compared with existing methods. The paper attributed these gains mainly to multi-spectral feature learning, which allowed the model to learn more discriminative features from different frequency and spatial resolutions.
[context]
The experimental setting is directly relevant to the proposed TUSZ v1.5.2 benchmark and cross-patient seizure-classification plan because it uses the same v1.5.2 corpus, evaluates seizure-type classification under patient-disjoint conditions, and reports weighted F1 score. The paper’s v1.5.2 experiments involved 3,050 annotated seizures, seven selected seizure types with at least three patients per type, and 3-fold patient-wise cross-validation. The v1.4.0 experiments involved 2,012 seizures and used seizure-wise 5-fold cross-validation due to limited patient counts for two seizure types. Hardware details were not reported. This paper provides a spectrogram-based ensemble CNN baseline for cross-patient seizure classification, which can be compared against the proposed invariant spatiotemporal representation learning approach that uses raw EEG, patient-group environments, self-supervised learning, and invariant risk minimization on the same TUSZ v1.5.2 dataset.
[end related work 2]

