[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Saputro et al., 2019-05)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ab9a19e288edd06d809cdd51a2c8783cba526bae)
- venue: Journal of Physics, Conference Series
- citation count: 82
[method]
The paper used a classical feature-based seizure classification pipeline on the Temple University Hospital Seizure Corpus. It extracted three Hjorth descriptors (activity, mobility, complexity), 14 MFCC coefficients, and an ICA-derived weight vector. ICA was applied as blind source separation under independence and non-Gaussianity assumptions; components with nonzero kurtosis were retained, the component with the highest kurtosis was selected, and its weight vector was used as a feature. Five feature scenarios were tested: Hjorth only (3 features), MFCC only (14 features), MFCC plus Hjorth (17 features), ICA plus Hjorth (4 features: weight vector, activity, mobility, complexity), and all three (18 features). The features were classified with support vector machines using linear, quadratic, cubic, fine Gaussian, medium Gaussian, and coarse Gaussian kernels. The evaluation used 5-fold cross-validation and a train/test split described elsewhere, with performance computed from TP, TN, FP, and FN. This method relates to the proposed TUSZ benchmark because it uses the same Temple University Hospital seizure corpus for seizure-type classification, but it is a non-deep, non-invariant baseline and does not implement the proposed cross-patient patient-disjoint evaluation, spatiotemporal invariant learning, or weighted F1 metric.
[results]
The best SVM kernel differed by feature scenario. For Hjorth only, the quadratic kernel achieved 81% accuracy. For MFCC only, MFCC plus Hjorth, ICA plus Hjorth, and all features, the cubic kernel achieved 88.60%, 91.40%, 84.30%, and 90.50% accuracy, respectively. The overall best result was the MFCC plus Hjorth scenario with the cubic kernel, reporting 91.4% accuracy, 90.25% average sensitivity, and 97.83% average specificity. No statistical significance tests or comparisons to deep-learning baselines were reported.
[context]
The paper used normalized seizure-type signals including GNSZ, FNSZ, TCSZ, and normal EEG, and stated that seizure signals visually showed lower frequency and higher amplitude. Experiments were run in Matlab R2018b using an MFCC toolbox and an SVM toolbox. The paper did not specify the TUSZ version, number of patients, electrode count, sampling rate, window length, or hardware. It also did not specify a patient-disjoint cross-patient split, although the proposed method uses TUSZ v1.5.2 with 5,612 signals, 3,050 annotated seizure events, over 300 patients, eight seizure types, 19 electrodes, 200 Hz resampling, 60-second windows, 1-second intervals, FFT log-amplitude features, and disjoint training/validation/test patient sets of 179, 22, and 34 patients. Therefore, the paper's results provide a classical same-corpus reference point but are not directly comparable to the proposed cross-patient invariant representation benchmark without additional matching of dataset version, split, preprocessing, and evaluation metric.
[end related work 3]

