[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Raghu et al., 2020-01)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d3da7fe00d8a1767726e865256b62b081b2b8630)
- venue: Neural Networks
- citation count: 329
[method]
The paper used a CNN-based EEG seizure-type classification pipeline. Raw 19-channel EEG signals were bandpass filtered at 0.1-44 Hz and converted into spectrogram images using short-time Fourier transforms with a Kaiser window of length 63, shape parameter 1, 75% overlap, and FFT length 256. Spectrograms from all 19 channels were vertically concatenated into a single image. Class imbalance was addressed using an overlap sampling technique to create balanced training samples. Two classification strategies were evaluated: first, transfer learning by fine-tuning pretrained image CNNs, including AlexNet, VGG16, VGG19, SqueezeNet, GoogLeNet, InceptionV3, DenseNet201, ResNet18, ResNet50, and ResNet101, using SGDm, RMSprop, and Adam with learning rates including 1e-3, 1e-4, and 1e-5; second, feature extraction from pretrained networks, where features from individual layers were extracted and classified using an SVM with a radial basis kernel. The dataset was split into 70% training and 30% testing, and experiments were repeated 10 times with averaged accuracy reported. This method relates to the proposed TUSZ benchmark and the overall research plan as a conventional CNN-based seizure-type classification approach that could serve as a baseline for multi-channel EEG seizure classification, but it does not implement the proposed cross-patient disjoint patient split, weighted F1 evaluation, self-supervised invariant mask, or invariant risk minimization.
[results]
Transfer learning achieved the highest accuracy of 82.85% using GoogLeNet with Adam and learning rate 1e-4. Other transfer-learning accuracies included AlexNet at 77.66% with SGDm and 1e-4, 76.60% with RMSprop and 1e-5, and 74.47% with Adam and 1e-4; VGG16 at 67.02% with RMSprop and 1e-5; VGG19 at 65.96% with RMSprop and 1e-5; GoogLeNet at 74.74% with SGDm and 1e-3, and 78.72% with RMSprop and 1e-4; InceptionV3 at 71.28%, 70.21%, and 68.21% with SGDm, RMSprop, and Adam respectively at 1e-3; and DenseNet201 at 75.53% with Adam and 1e-4. Feature extraction with SVM achieved the overall highest accuracy of 88.30% using InceptionV3 features from layers 37, 41, and 58. Other feature-extraction accuracies were AlexNet 76.60% from layers 13-16, VGG16 83.83% from layers 32 and 34, VGG19 81.82% from layer 39, SqueezeNet 85.11% from layer 59, GoogLeNet 85.11% from layer 56, DenseNet201 85.11% from layer 236, ResNet18 86.17% from layers 30 and 31, ResNet50 86.17% from layer 56, and ResNet101 87.23% from layers 101, 102, and 105. Across feature-extraction layers, ReLU layers generally produced the highest accuracies. GoogLeNet transfer-learning predictions showed high probabilities for TN and TC seizure types, and seizure types AB, FN, GN, CP, and SP had predicted probabilities above 80%.
[context]
The experimental context was a 19-channel EEG seizure-type classification task involving multiple seizure types, including TN, TC, AB, FN, GN, CP, and SP. The data were divided into 70% training and 30% testing, with 10 repeated runs and classification accuracy as the reported metric. No patient-disjoint split, cross-patient validation, weighted F1, or statistical significance testing was described. The main experimental variables were pretrained network choice, optimizer, learning rate, and extracted layer depth. The method is relevant to the proposed TUSZ v1.5.2 benchmark because it targets the same multi-channel EEG seizure-type classification problem and provides a CNN transfer-learning and feature-extraction baseline, but it is not directly aligned with the proposed cross-patient ST-IRM framework because it lacks patient-level disjoint evaluation, invariant representation learning, self-supervised temporal consistency, and the TUSZ-specific 1,925/450/521 clip split.
[end related work 4]

