[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6)
- venue: International Conference on Learning Representations
- citation count: 175
[method]
The paper uses the Temple University Hospital EEG Seizure Corpus v1.5.2 as a multi-patient EEG benchmark, matching the proposed method's dataset choice. It preprocesses raw 19-channel standard 10-20 EEGs into frequency-domain inputs by taking log-amplitudes of the fast Fourier transform. For seizure detection and self-supervised pre-training, it uses both seizure and non-seizure EEGs in non-overlapping 12-second and 60-second clips; for seizure classification, it uses one 12-second or 60-second clip per seizure event. It trains a distance-graph or correlation-graph DCRNN graph neural network, with and without self-supervised pre-training, and evaluates it against Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines trained on the same preprocessed data. The setup is directly relevant to the proposed ST-IRM plan because it uses the same TUSZ v1.5.2 benchmark, patient-disjoint splits, frequency-domain EEG clips, and several of the baselines named in the research plan.
[results]
On TUSZ, DCRNN without self-supervised pre-training performed on par with or better than the baselines, and self-supervised pre-training improved both seizure detection and classification. The pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN achieved an AUROC of 0.875 for 60-second seizure detection, matching a CNN with AUROC 0.88 that had been pre-trained on a labeled dataset five times larger than TUSZ. For 12-second seizure detection at 25% false positive rate, pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN achieved 84.3% true positive rate and pre-trained correlation-graph DCRNN achieved 81.7%, compared with 72.8% for Dense-CNN, 67.0% for LSTM, and 62.5% for CNN-LSTM. For 12-second seizure classification, non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN achieved 93% accuracy on rare absence seizures, 2 points above the best baseline; pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN achieved 74% accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, 47 points above Dense-CNN and 48 points above non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN. Frequency-domain inputs substantially outperformed time-domain inputs. Statistical significance was not reported in the provided excerpt. These results are relevant to the research plan because they show that graph-based and self-supervised approaches can improve cross-patient seizure detection and rare-class classification on the same TUSZ benchmark.
[context]
The experimental population was the TUSZ v1.5.2 corpus with 5,612 EEGs and 3,050 annotated seizures from clinical recordings, using 19 EEG channels from the standard 10-20 system. Five patients present in both the official TUSZ train and test sets were excluded to ensure unseen-patient evaluation. The official TUSZ train set was split by patients into 90% training and 10% validation, and the official test set was held out, so train, validation, and test patients were distinct. The classification scheme was refined to four classes: combined focal, generalized non-specific, absence, and combined tonic, because focal non-specific, simple partial, and complex partial seizures were not distinguishable by EEG alone and tonic/tonic-clonic seizures were rare. Evaluation used AUROC for detection and weighted F1-score for classification, although the provided excerpt reports AUROC, true positive rate at fixed false positive rate, and class accuracy rather than full weighted F1 values. Hardware and detailed hyperparameters were not reported in the provided sections. This context supports the proposed benchmark and the cross-patient evaluation design in the research plan, but the paper's specific class merging and clip construction differ from the plan's 1-second interval segmentation and 179/22/34 patient split.
[end related work 5]

