[method]
The paper evaluates MP-SeizNet, a multi-path deep learning classifier for specific seizure-type classification from scalp EEG. It uses the TUSZ v1.5.2 corpus, selects a 20-channel transverse central parietal montage, resamples recordings to 250 Hz, and crops them into non-overlapping 2-second segments. The model combines a CNN sub-model and an attention-based Bi-LSTM sub-model, and is trained with cross-entropy loss, the Adam optimizer, batch size 64, up to 100 epochs, early stopping after 10 epochs without validation-loss improvement, and a learning rate reduced from 0.01 to 1e-4. Evaluation uses weighted F1-score as the primary metric, with sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, negative predictive value, and AUC computed from confusion matrices. Two evaluation protocols are used: seizure-wise 5-fold stratified cross-validation with 80:20 train/validation splits, and patient-wise 3-fold cross-validation where each fold contains data from different patients. This is directly related to the proposed benchmark and research plan because it uses the same TUSZ v1.5.2 multi-patient seizure EEG corpus, evaluates seizure-type classification under a patient-independent setting, and reports weighted F1, providing a comparable deep-learning baseline and validation context for cross-patient seizure classification.
[results]
In patient-wise 3-fold cross-validation, MP-SeizNet achieved a weighted F1-score of 87.62%, outperforming its CNN and Bi-LSTM-attention sub-models by more than 1 to 3 percentage points. In seizure-wise 5-fold cross-validation, MP-SeizNet achieved a weighted F1-score of 98.01%, while the CNN sub-model was within 0.1 percentage point of this result. The attention-based Bi-LSTM sub-model achieved 97.04% weighted F1, and the paper reports that adding attention improved performance by more than 10 percentage points relative to a Bi-LSTM without attention. The paper also reports 95% confidence intervals from 20 randomized tests as 0.975-0.977 for an F1-score of 0.976 and 0.961-0.965 for an F1-score of 0.963, but it does not explicitly state which interval corresponds to the seizure-wise versus patient-wise protocol. No p-values or formal significance tests are reported in the provided text beyond these confidence intervals.
