The experiments used TUSZ v1.5.2, a hospital EEG corpus collected from 2002 to 2012 with more than 300 patients and more than 3000 seizure events. Although the corpus contains eight seizure labels, the paper excludes nonspecific labels and myoclonic seizures because the nonspecific labels are not fully disjoint from specific labels and myoclonic seizures have only three cases from two patients. The EEG data were recorded at 250-512 Hz using the 10-20 system; the paper used 20 transverse central parietal channels, 250 Hz resampling, and 2-second non-overlapping segments. For patient-wise evaluation, 3-fold cross-validation was used because the selected seizure classes were recorded from at least three patients, and when a test class had substantially more data than the training data, a subset of test data covering the start, middle, and end of the event was selected. The provided text does not specify hardware, software versions, or exact patient counts for each fold. These conditions matter for the proposed method because the proposed plan also targets TUSZ v1.5.2 and cross-patient evaluation, but it uses 19 electrodes, 200 Hz resampling, 60-second windows, 1-second FFT clips, and a fixed 179/22/34 patient split, so the paper's results are comparable in dataset and metric but not directly reproducible under the proposed preprocessing and split.
