[method]
The paper describes a frequency-domain EEG analysis method in which continuous or epoch-averaged EEG data are transformed into spectral representations to compute baseline or epoch-mean power spectra and event-related time/frequency measures: event-related spectral perturbation, inter-trial coherence magnitude and phase, and event-related cross-coherence magnitude and phase. These measures are computed for single electrodes or for independent components obtained by infomax ICA or other ICA algorithms. The ICA step decomposes EEG into independent components with scalp maps, activity time courses, spectra, and ERP images, and can use PCA preprocessing when electrode activities are linearly dependent. This is related to the proposed method because both convert EEG into frequency-domain features, but the paper uses event-related spectral perturbation and coherence rather than the proposed 200 Hz resampling, 60-second non-overlapping windows, 1-second FFT windows, and logarithmic non-negative frequency amplitudes. It is related to the research plan only as a source/spectral decomposition context for separating neural and artifactual or patient-specific components, not as an invariant risk minimization or cross-patient seizure classifier.
[results]
No quantitative classification performance, baseline comparison, or statistical significance is reported for the time/frequency method. The paper provides illustrative ICA results on a 32-channel sample dataset containing 80 EEG epochs: the ICA algorithm returned 32 components, lower-index components accounted for more EEG variance, and one far-frontal component was identified as an eye-movement artifact based on its scalp map, smoothly decreasing activity spectrum, and prominent eye artifacts in its ERP image. Component contribution visualizations showed alpha-frequency contributions at channel POz and ERP contributions in the -500 to 1000 ms latency range. No F1, precision, recall, accuracy, or seizure-type classification outcomes are reported.
[context]
The experimental conditions are limited to a sample EEG dataset and toolbox functionality: 32 channels, 80 epochs, ICA components, single-channel or component-level spectral measures, and event-related latency windows. ICA assumptions include sufficient data points for temporal independence, no linear mixture of electrode activities, spatial stationarity of sources, and non-Gaussian source distributions, with extended infomax for sub-Gaussian sources. No hardware, computational resources, hyperparameters, patient population, or evaluation protocol are specified. The proposed TUSZ plan differs by using 19 electrodes, 200 Hz sampling, 60-second clips, 1-second FFT intervals, disjoint patient splits of 179, 22, and 34 patients, weighted F1 as the primary metric, and invariant representation learning across patient groups.
