[method]
The paper implements a binary seizure-detection pipeline on clinical EEG. Recordings sampled at 200 Hz are bandpass filtered from 1.6 to 50 Hz and notched at 60 Hz, then segmented into 1-second windows with 250 ms overlap. Two emission features are computed from common-reference channels: log-summed short-time Fourier transform magnitudes in the theta, delta, alpha, and beta bands, and log line length. These features are used by a coupled hidden Markov model with a transition prior and compared against logistic regression, polynomial-kernel support vector machines, and a Gaussian mixture model likelihood-ratio baseline. This is related to the proposed method because both use 200 Hz EEG, 1-second temporal units, and frequency-domain log-amplitude features, but the paper does not use non-overlapping 60-second clips, full non-negative FFT amplitudes, patient-disjoint cross-patient evaluation, or invariant spatiotemporal representation learning.
[results]
The provided excerpt reports no numerical accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, or F1 values. Qualitatively, the transition prior in the coupled hidden Markov model increases confidence over contiguous seizure-like regions and can indicate focal right temporal activity, but it reduces specificity by associating post-seizure spectral artifacts with seizure. The logistic regression and Gaussian mixture model baselines classify portions of seizure intervals but lack consistency, with the Gaussian mixture model appearing more confident than logistic regression. The polynomial-kernel support vector machine performs poorly because the EEG features are described as inseparable in the noisy clinical dataset.
[context]
The dataset consisted of 90 seizure recordings from 15 patients, with up to 10 minutes of baseline before and after each seizure, sampled at 200 Hz and validated using expert clinical annotations of seizure onset and offset. Preprocessing used fourth-order Butterworth high-pass and low-pass filters at 1.6 Hz and 50 Hz, a second-order 60 Hz notch filter with Q = 20, and a Tukey window with shape parameter 0.25 for the short-time Fourier transform. Spectral features were computed for theta 1-4 Hz, delta 4-8 Hz, alpha 8-13 Hz, and beta 13-30 Hz. Evaluation used 5-fold cross-validation with recordings randomly assigned to folds independently of patient, and metrics included average test-fold accuracy, sensitivity, and specificity, with probabilistic classifier rates weighted by posterior confidence. This setup differs from the research plan, which targets cross-patient seizure-type classification on the TUSZ dataset using patient-disjoint training, validation, and test sets.
