- venue: Frontiers in Neurology
- citation count: 41
[method]
The paper used a single-lead ECG-based seizure detection pipeline in which heart rate was extracted from a bipolar ECG channel recorded alongside 10-20 scalp EEG. A patient-independent classifier was first trained on pooled data, and then personalized through transfer learning by regularizing a new patient-specific classifier toward the patient-independent model. The transfer-learning formulation included a weight parameter D controlling how strongly the personalized model could deviate from the patient-independent model and a gamma parameter controlling the classifier margin or penalty. The paper compared patient-independent, fully patient-specific, mixed, and transfer-learning personalization strategies, and evaluated personalization with 0 to 4 randomly selected patient-specific seizures over 100 simulations, where 0 seizures corresponded to the patient-independent model. This method relates to the proposed TUSZ EEG frequency-domain preprocessing and invariant spatiotemporal learning plan at the level of cross-patient seizure detection and adaptation to patient-specific variability, although the paper uses ECG heart-rate features for binary seizure detection rather than EEG FFT amplitudes for seizure-type classification.
[results]
The patient-independent approach achieved an average sensitivity of 75%, a false detection rate of 3.0 false positives per hour, and an F3 score of 0.22. The transfer-learning approach achieved a sensitivity of 71%, a false detection rate of 1.9 false positives per hour, and an F3 score of 0.30, corresponding to a 37% reduction in false positives relative to the patient-independent approach. The sensitivity difference between patient-independent and transfer-learning models was not statistically significant, with p = 0.29, while the false detection rate was significantly lower for transfer learning, with p < 0.001. The mixed personalization approach had similar sensitivity but produced on average 0.7 false positives per hour more than transfer learning, with a significant false detection rate difference of p = 0.03. The fully patient-specific approach had lower sensitivity than transfer learning, with p = 0.01, and a false detection rate of 2.2 false positives per hour that was not significantly different from transfer learning, with p = 0.29. The F3 scores for the patient-independent, fully patient-specific, and mixed approaches were all significantly lower than transfer learning, with p < 0.001, p = 0.01, and p < 0.01, respectively. The average detection delay for the transfer-learning approach was 21 seconds. Preprocessing identified 84.7% of seizures as having ictal heart-rate increases. In the number-of-seizures simulation, one patient-specific seizure produced a strong F3 improvement over the patient-independent baseline, two seizures reduced result variability, and performance continued to improve up to four seizures. Alternative personalization methods remained lower than transfer learning across all tested numbers of training seizures.
[context]
The study used 24 refractory epilepsy patients with 2,172 hours of continuous inpatient recordings and 228 annotated temporal-lobe seizures. Recordings included 10-20 scalp EEG and one bipolar ECG lead II at 250 Hz. ECG segments that were unreadable for 4.6, 8.8, and 7.4 hours in three patients were removed. Seizure onsets and offsets were annotated by a clinical expert using video-EEG without considering ECG and then validated by a neurologist, but seizure offsets were unavailable for 30% of seizures. A detection was counted as true if it occurred between 1 minute before EEG seizure onset and 2 minutes after onset, and false detections within 1 minute of each other were merged into one false detection. Performance was reported as patient-average unless otherwise stated, and the F3 score used beta = 3 to weight sensitivity more heavily than false detections. Statistical comparisons used paired t-tests, and all results were obtained retrospectively in a simulation replicating a real-time setting. The transfer-learning classifier was illustrated with D values of 0.01 and 1 and a fixed gamma value of 1.5, where low D kept the personalized model close to the patient-independent boundary and high D allowed stronger adaptation to patient-specific data. The provided sections did not report computational hardware, software, or training time.
