[method]
The paper uses a frequency-domain EEG preprocessing and ConvLSTM seizure-detection pipeline that is related to the proposed FFT-based preparation of TUSZ clips for invariant spatiotemporal learning. Raw EEG was split into 12-second segments, cleaned with independent component analysis using 19 components, and eye-movement artifacts were removed by correlating components with FP1 and FP2. A short-time Fourier transform was then applied with a window length of 250 samples, described as 1 second, and 50% overlap, producing a 23-electrode by 125-frequency spectrotemporal representation. These inputs were processed by a three-layer ConvLSTM with 16, 32, and 64 filters, followed by fully connected layers, batch normalization, dropout, and a seizure classification output. The model was trained on the Temple University Hospital EEG corpus and run in inference-only mode on the Royal Prince Alfred Hospital corpus. A real-time lens post-processing step used periodic waveform and energy indices in delta, theta, alpha, beta, and gamma bands, with 85th-percentile adaptive thresholds over the previous two hours, to refine high-risk AI detections. This is related to the proposed method because both use frequency-domain EEG features for seizure-related learning, but the paper uses 12-second STFT segments with overlap and 23 channels for seizure detection, whereas the proposal uses non-overlapped 60-second TUSZ clips split into 1-second FFT intervals with 19 channels and logarithmic amplitudes for cross-patient seizure-type classification.
[results]
On the unseen Royal Prince Alfred Hospital inference set, the system achieved an AUC of 0.82, 76.68% sensitivity, and an average of 56.22 false alarms per 24 hours in the text, with a figure value of 56.55 false alarms per 24 hours. In a 66-session clinical pilot with an expert human arbiter, the AI-assisted pathway achieved 92.19% sensitivity, matching the conventional human review pathway, while reducing review time from 90 to 120 minutes per 24-hour session to about 7.62 minutes. In the same clinical test, the conventional Encevis tool achieved 62.50% sensitivity and 7.02 false alarms per 24 hours, so the paper's system traded a higher false-alarm rate for higher sensitivity and relied on human arbitration to reject false alarms. On the Temple University Hospital corpus, the paper reported a 6% average AUC improvement over a comparable prior method while using 12-second windows instead of 60-second windows.
[context]
The training corpus contained 1,185 sessions from 592 patients, including 202 seizure patients, 923 hours, and 2,370 seizures, corresponding to 46.7 seizure hours or 6.2% of total duration; the development set contained 238 sessions from 50 patients, including 40 seizure patients. The inference corpus contained 1,006 sessions from 212 initially identified patients, with 20 patients excluded, leaving 192 included patients, 14,590 hours, 536 seizures, and 29.9 seizure hours, or 0.2% of total duration, recorded from 2011 to 2019. The Temple University Hospital data were recorded with Natus NicoletOne hardware and the Royal Prince Alfred Hospital data with Compumedics hardware. The STFT window of 250 samples is described as 1 second, suggesting an approximately 250 Hz sampling rate for that transform, unlike the proposed 200 Hz resampling. The clinical pilot used 66 randomly selected sessions reviewed by two neurologists and three EEG technicians to establish ground truth, and the AI-assisted review used a graphical user interface for accepting or rejecting seizure onsets and offsets. These conditions are relevant to the research plan because they demonstrate frequency-domain EEG representation learning and cross-site generalization, but they differ from the proposed TUSZ v1.5.2 setup in dataset version, electrode count, window length, overlap, task, and evaluation metric, since the paper evaluates seizure detection with AUC, sensitivity, and false alarms rather than weighted F1 for eight seizure types.
