[method]
The paper evaluated methodological choices for binary seizure/non-seizure EEG detection using the CHB-MIT dataset, with particular emphasis on data segmentation, temporal data dependencies, and evaluation metrics. It compared several data-preparation strategies: balanced Fact1 and 10:1 Fact10 subsets containing all seizure data plus selected non-seizure data, and full-data temporal files organized seizure-to-seizure or in fixed 1-hour and 4-hour windows. For full-data temporal training, time-series cross-validation was used, with the first file required to contain at least five hours of data and one seizure. For subset-based experiments, both leave-one-seizure-out and time-series cross-validation were compared. The segmentation experiment used fixed 4-second feature-extraction windows with moving step sizes ranging from 0.5 to 4 seconds. Performance was evaluated using sample-based EPOCH metrics, episode-based OVLP metrics, sensitivity, precision, F1 score, and false alarms per day. Overall cross-validation performance was discussed both as an average over folds and as temporally appended predictions. This relates to the proposed TUSZ method because the plan also relies on fixed EEG windowing, specifically non-overlapped 60-second clips further divided into 1-second FFT intervals, and because the overall research plan requires careful cross-patient evaluation and realistic performance estimation.
[results]
Personalized models trained on the balanced CHB-MIT subset achieved approximately 100% episode-level sensitivity on average, but duration-level sensitivity was lower, and precision was reduced by many short false positives. Full-data temporal training reduced false positives compared with Fact1 and Fact10 subsets and produced lower but more realistic sensitivity and precision values. Subset-based false alarm rates were substantially higher and were judged unrealistic for estimating final false alarm performance. Leave-one-seizure-out outperformed time-series cross-validation by about 3% to 7% in episode-level F1 score, demonstrating that training on future data can overestimate performance. In the windowing experiment, increasing the step size from 0.5 to 4 seconds with a fixed 4-second window reduced the number of false positives, but increased the proportion of false positives, causing precision to rise initially and then fall. Sensitivity also decreased with larger step sizes, especially at the duration level, indicating that very large steps may miss shorter seizure patterns. No statistical significance tests or direct baseline model comparisons were reported in the provided text.
[context]
The experiments used CHB-MIT, containing 982.9 hours of 256-Hz EEG from 24 subjects, 183 seizures totaling 3.2 hours, or 0.32% ictal data, with 18 common channels used. The models were personalized per subject rather than cross-patient, and the exact classifier architecture and computational hardware were not specified in the provided text. The segmentation experiment used a 4-second window and step sizes from 0.5 to 4 seconds, while the proposed TUSZ method uses non-overlapped 60-second windows and 1-second FFT intervals. This is relevant because the paper shows that window size and step size materially affect false positives, precision, and sensitivity, and notes that frequency-domain features may require larger windows to capture relevant information. The paper also supports using full temporal data for realistic false-alarm and precision estimates, avoiding non-temporal cross-validation when online or prospective deployment is intended, and reporting both episode-level and duration-level metrics. These findings are directly relevant to the proposed cross-patient TUSZ study, although the paper’s binary CHB-MIT personalized results are not a direct substitute for the plan’s multi-class cross-patient weighted F1 evaluation.
