[method]
The paper used a patient-independent seizure detection framework to evaluate generalization to unseen patients. A seizure detector was trained on the SeizeIT1 dataset, which contained full-scalp EEG and behind-the-ear EEG from 42 patients, and then evaluated on the separate SeizeIT2 dataset, which contained full-scalp EEG and behind-the-ear EEG from 39 different patients. Because the training dataset had three behind-the-ear channels and the test dataset had only two, one unilateral channel was removed and the detector was retrained on the two channels selected according to the hemisphere where epileptic activity was most visible. The pipeline used 1–25 Hz bandpass filtering, 2-second EEG segments with 50% overlap, feature extraction, normalization, an SVM classifier, and post-processing based on root mean square amplitude thresholds and a rule requiring 8 of 10 consecutive windows to be classified as seizure. Three training conditions were compared: original full-scalp EEG annotations, visually corrected behind-the-ear annotations, and automatically corrected behind-the-ear annotations. This relates to the proposed method because both use patient-disjoint data to assess cross-patient generalization, although the paper performs binary seizure detection on two separate datasets rather than seizure-type classification on a single TUSZ dataset split into training, validation, and test sets.
[results]
On the 39-patient SeizeIT2 test set, the model trained with original full-scalp EEG annotations achieved 85.4% sensitivity and 6.32 false alarms per hour. Retraining with visually corrected behind-the-ear annotations improved performance to 86.3% sensitivity and 2.31 false alarms per hour. Retraining with automatically corrected annotations achieved the best reported performance, with 87.5% sensitivity and 1.93 false alarms per hour. Patient-level performance was highly variable: some patients had 100% sensitivity, while others had 0%, including patient 15, whose only seizure was short in duration and low in amplitude.
[context]
SeizeIT1 contained 4034 hours of EEG from 42 patients and was used for training. SeizeIT2 contained 3199.9 hours of EEG from 39 patients and was used to evaluate performance on a fully wearable device. Recordings were sampled at 250 Hz. SeizeIT1 used full-scalp 10-20 EEG plus behind-the-ear channels recorded by vEEG, with three behind-the-ear channels retained: unilateral right, unilateral left, and crosshead 2. SeizeIT2 used extended 10-20 full-scalp EEG plus two behind-the-ear channels recorded by a wearable Sensor Dot device, with monitoring durations ranging from 18.6 to 117.3 hours. Evaluation was patient-independent and event-based, using seizure detection sensitivity and false alarm rate per hour. This context supports the proposed cross-patient evaluation plan by demonstrating that disjoint patient cohorts can be used to test generalization, but the paper differs from the proposed TUSZ experiment in dataset, channel count, clip length, classification task, and evaluation metrics.
