[method]
The paper evaluates a graph neural network called DCRNN for cross-patient EEG seizure detection and classification on the TUSZ v1.5.2 dataset. Raw 19-channel EEG signals are transformed into frequency-domain features using log-amplitudes of the fast Fourier transform and segmented into non-overlapping 12-second and 60-second clips. Two graph-based DCRNN variants are tested: a distance-graph DCRNN and a correlation-graph DCRNN. These are compared against conventional baselines trained and evaluated on the same preprocessed data: Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM. A self-supervised pre-training stage is also applied to DCRNN, and the resulting pre-trained weights are reused for both seizure detection and seizure classification. The classification task uses a refined four-class scheme consisting of combined focal, generalized non-specific, absence, and combined tonic seizures, with one clip per seizure event. This method is directly related to the proposed research method because it provides the Dist-DCRNN and Corr-DCRNN GNN baselines and a self-supervised pre-training comparison under a patient-disjoint TUSZ setup, although it does not include MSTGCN, NeuroGNN, or PANN-DCRNN.
[results]
Without self-supervised pre-training, distance-graph DCRNN and correlation-graph DCRNN perform on par with or better than Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM, and DCRNN outperforms two reported CNN seizure-classification results. Self-supervised pre-training improves both seizure detection and classification. For 60-second seizure detection, pre-trained distance-DCRNN reaches an AUROC of 0.875, matching a CNN with AUROC 0.88 that was pre-trained on a labeled dataset five times larger than TUSZ. On 12-second seizure detection at a 25% false positive rate, pre-trained distance-DCRNN achieves 84.3% true positive rate, pre-trained correlation-DCRNN achieves 81.7%, Dense-CNN achieves 72.8%, LSTM achieves 67.0%, and CNN-LSTM achieves 62.5%. For 12-second seizure classification, non-pretrained distance-DCRNN achieves 93% accuracy on rare absence seizures, which is 2 points higher than the best baseline. Pretrained distance-DCRNN achieves 74% accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, which is 47 points higher than Dense-CNN and 48 points higher than non-pretrained distance-DCRNN. Manual review of 32 misclassified generalized non-specific test seizures found that 27 were actually focal seizures mislabeled as generalized, while 5 were true generalized seizures misclassified by the model. The provided text does not report the full weighted F1-score table, so the strongest quantitative evidence available is the AUROC and per-class accuracy values above.
[context]
Experiments use TUSZ v1.5.2 with 5,612 EEGs, 3,050 annotated seizures, eight original seizure types, and 19 standard 10-20 system channels. To evaluate generalization to unseen patients, the official train set is split 90/10 by patient into training and validation sets, the official test set is held out, and five patients appearing in both the official train and test sets are excluded, so the train, validation, and test sets contain distinct patients. Seizure detection uses both seizure and non-seizure EEGs, while seizure classification uses only seizure EEGs with one 12-second or 60-second clip per seizure. Myoclonic seizures are excluded because only three examples are available, and tonic and tonic-clonic seizures are combined because tonic and tonic-clonic seizures are rare and tonic-clonic seizures begin with a tonic phase. Primary metrics are AUROC for seizure detection and weighted F1-score for seizure classification. The provided text does not report hardware or full hyperparameter values, but the setup is relevant to the proposed cross-patient benchmark because it uses frequency-domain 12-second and 60-second clips, patient-disjoint splits, and weighted F1 evaluation.
