[method]
The paper evaluates SeizureNet, an ensemble of deep CNNs that classifies seizure types from raw EEG after converting signals into saliency-encoded spectrograms and applying multi-spectral feature learning. It is relevant to the proposed cross-patient benchmark because it is a CNN-based seizure-type classifier evaluated with patient-disjoint validation on TUH-EEGSC v1.5.2 and reports weighted F1, the same primary metric used in the research plan. The model is trained by minimizing cross-entropy over ensemble logits, where ensemble logits are averaged across sub-networks and passed through softmax. Inputs are spectrogram clips derived from EEG; the method does not use GNNs, invariant risk minimization, or explicit patient-environment partitioning, so it serves as a conventional CNN baseline rather than the proposed ST-IRM method.
[results]
On TUH-EEGSC, SeizureNet improved patient-wise weighted F1 score by about 3 percentage points and seizure-wise weighted F1 score by about 4 percentage points compared with existing methods. The excerpt does not provide absolute F1 values, per-class metrics, confidence intervals, or statistical significance tests. The improvement is attributed to multi-spectral feature learning capturing different frequency and spatial resolutions.
[context]
Experiments used TUH-EEGSC v1.4.0 with 2012 seizures and TUH-EEGSC v1.5.2 with 3050 seizures. Myoclonic seizures were excluded because only three examples were available. For v1.4.0, 5-fold seizure-wise cross-validation was used because TN and SP types had data from only two patients. For v1.5.2, 3-fold patient-wise cross-validation was used because the seven selected seizure types each had data from at least three patients, ensuring train and test seizures came from different patients. Training used 400 epochs, Adam optimizer, batch size 50, initial learning rate 0.001 reduced by factor 10 at 50% and 75% of epochs, weight decay 0.0005, weights initialized from zero-mean Gaussian with standard deviation 0.01, biases zero, and Torch automatic differentiation. Hardware details are not provided.
