- in_text_citation: [(O'Shea et al., 2019-11)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/92988cf1e5b6bf7d876a0a0151211ea60dbb0746)
- venue: Neural Networks
- citation count: 151
[method]
The paper evaluated two fully convolutional network (FCN) baselines for patient-independent neonatal seizure detection from raw EEG. The 1D FCN processed a single EEG channel as a 256-sample 8-second window at 32 Hz, used convolutional, pooling, and batch normalization blocks with 32 feature maps, a final convolutional classification layer, and global pooling, and required strong channel-specific seizure labels. The 2D FCN processed multiple channels as a 256-by-N array, used only 1D convolutions, added maximum pooling across channels to learn whether any channel contained seizure activity, and was trained with weak labels over full recordings. Both models were trained with leave-one-baby-out cross-validation on the Cork dataset and then tested once on the Helsinki dataset. This relates to the proposed method because it provides a CNN-based cross-patient EEG seizure detection benchmark and patient-disjoint evaluation, analogous to the proposed cross-patient seizure classification setup, although it targets binary seizure detection rather than multi-class seizure-type classification and does not include RNN or GNN baselines.
[results]
For the Cork leave-one-out experiments, the 1D FCN improved the per-baby AUC over the SVM baseline in almost every baby, with an average absolute AUC increase of more than 1 percentage point. On concatenated data, the reported deep learning result achieved an AUC of 98.5 compared with 96.6 for the SVM baseline, corresponding to a 56% improvement over the achievable margin. The 2D FCN trained with weak labels was reported as the best-performing model on both the Cork leave-one-out test and the unseen Helsinki test, and it had the smallest performance drop between matched and mismatched dataset conditions. The paper did not provide exact AUC values for the 2D FCN in the excerpt. Processing speed was also reported: the 1D and 2D FCNs processed one hour of EEG in under one minute on a CPU, whereas the Helsinki SVM took over 4.5 hours for one hour of EEG.
[context]
The Cork dataset contained 18 full-term hypoxic-ischemic encephalopathy babies, 834 hours of 8-channel EEG at 256 Hz, and 1,389 consensus-annotated seizures. The Helsinki test dataset contained 79 full-term babies, 112 hours of 18-channel EEG at 256 Hz, and 342 seizures, with 39 babies having seizures. Preprocessing included band-pass filtering from 0.5 to 12.8 Hz and downsampling to 32 Hz; FCN inputs were 8-second windows with a 1-second shift. The 1D FCN used strong labels, early stopping after 25 non-improving validation iterations, stochastic gradient descent with learning rate 0.001, Nesterov momentum 0.9, batch size 4096, and categorical cross-entropy. The 2D FCN used weak labels, split 14 training and 3 validation babies per leave-one-out fold, repeated three random validation splits, early stopped after 8 non-improving iterations, averaged probabilities from three networks, used batch size 300, and binary cross-entropy. Evaluation used AUC and AUC90, not weighted F1, and the cross-dataset test compared algorithms trained on Cork against an algorithm trained on Helsinki, providing a mismatched generalization condition similar to the proposed cross-patient distribution-shift setting.
