[method]
The paper evaluates a graph neural network called DCRNN for cross-patient EEG seizure detection and classification on TUSZ v1.5.2. It constructs two graph variants: Dist-DCRNN using a distance-based graph over 19 standard 10-20 channels and Corr-DCRNN using a correlation-based graph. Inputs are log-amplitude fast Fourier transform features from raw EEG, segmented into non-overlapping 12-s and 60-s clips for detection and self-supervised pre-training, and one 12-s or 60-s clip per seizure event for classification. The classification task uses a refined four-class scheme: combined focal, generalized non-specific, absence, and combined tonic. The model is compared with Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines trained on the same preprocessed data. A self-supervised pre-training stage is also tested, where DCRNN is first trained on unlabeled seizure and non-seizure clips and then fine-tuned for downstream detection and classification. This is directly relevant to the proposed benchmark because it uses the same TUSZ v1.5.2 corpus, patient-disjoint evaluation, frequency-domain 60-s inputs, weighted F1/AUROC evaluation, and includes several baselines named in the proposed method, namely DenseCNN, LSTM, CNN-LSTM, Dist-DCRNN, and Corr-DCRNN; it does not include MSTGCN, NeuroGNN, PANN-DCRNN, or the proposed ST-IRM invariant risk minimization framework.
[results]
Without self-supervised pre-training, Dist-DCRNN and Corr-DCRNN perform on par with or better than Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM on seizure detection and classification, and DCRNN outperforms reported CNN results on seizure classification. With self-supervised pre-training, DCRNN improves on both tasks. For 60-s seizure detection, pre-trained Dist-DCRNN reaches AUROC 0.875, matching a CNN pre-trained on a labeled dataset five times larger than TUSZ. For 12-s detection at 25% false positive rate, pre-trained Dist-DCRNN achieves 84.3% true positive rate, pre-trained Corr-DCRNN 81.7%, Dense-CNN 72.8%, LSTM 67.0%, and CNN-LSTM 62.5%. For 12-s classification, non-pretrained Dist-DCRNN achieves 93% accuracy on rare absence seizures, 2 points above the best baseline. Pretrained Dist-DCRNN achieves 74% accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, 47 points above Dense-CNN and 48 points above non-pretrained Dist-DCRNN. Many generalized non-specific seizures are misclassified as focal; manual review of 32 misclassified test generalized non-specific seizures found 27 were actually focal seizures mislabeled as generalized non-specific and 5 were true generalized seizures misclassified by the model. No statistical significance tests are reported in the provided text.
[context]
Experiments use TUSZ v1.5.2 with 5,612 EEGs, 3,050 annotated seizures, eight original seizure types, and 19 EEG channels. The official TUSZ train set is split by patients into 90% training and 10% validation, and the official TUSZ test set is held out after removing five patients who appear in both official train and test sets, so train, validation, and test patients are distinct. Preprocessing transforms raw EEG to log-amplitude FFT features; detection and self-supervised pre-training use both seizure and non-seizure clips, while classification uses only seizure clips with one clip per seizure event. The paper reports AUROC for detection and weighted F1-score as the main classification metric, but the provided results emphasize AUROC, true positive rate at fixed false positive rate, and per-class accuracy. Hardware, exact hyperparameters, and training epochs are not specified in the provided sections. The classification results are for the refined four-class scheme rather than the original eight TUSZ seizure types, which is an important constraint when comparing to the proposed plan that mentions eight seizure types.
