[method]
The paper evaluates a multi-path deep learning classifier for specific seizure-type classification on the TUSZ v1.5.2 EEG dataset, comprising a CNN sub-model, an attention-based Bi-LSTM sub-model, and a combined MP-SeizNet model. The input is scalp EEG from a 20-channel transverse central parietal montage, resampled to 250 Hz and cropped into non-overlapping 2-second segments. The model is evaluated under two protocols: seizure-wise stratified five-fold cross-validation and patient-wise three-fold cross-validation with patient-disjoint folds, making the patient-wise protocol directly relevant to the proposed cross-patient benchmark. The CNN and Bi-LSTM components correspond to the CNN-based and RNN-based/hybrid baseline categories in the proposed method, although the paper does not use the exact named baselines such as DenseCNN, MSTGCN, or DCRNN variants, and it does not implement invariant risk minimization or GNN-based spatiotemporal modeling. The results section also attributes part of the performance to wavelet-based features, although the provided excerpt does not detail their computation.
[results]
In the patient-wise cross-patient setting, MP-SeizNet achieved a weighted F1-score of 87.62%, outperforming the CNN sub-model and the Bi-LSTM-attention sub-model by a margin reported as more than 1 to 3 percentage points. In the seizure-wise setting, MP-SeizNet achieved a weighted F1-score of 98.01%, while the CNN sub-model was within 0.1 percentage point of this result. The attention-based Bi-LSTM sub-model achieved a weighted F1-score of 97.04%, and the paper reports that adding attention improved performance by more than 10 percentage points relative to a Bi-LSTM without attention. The paper also reports 95% confidence intervals from 20 randomized tests as 0.975 to 0.977 and 0.961 to 0.965, and states that the model achieved high sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV, and AUC values, although the exact per-matrix values are not included in the provided excerpt.
[context]
Experimental conditions include TUSZ v1.5.2, a multi-class hospital EEG seizure dataset with more than 300 patients and more than 3000 seizure events; the study excludes nonspecific focal/generalized labels and myoclonic seizures, leaving specific seizure types. Preprocessing uses a 20-channel TCP montage, 250 Hz resampling, and 2-second non-overlapping segments, which differs from the proposed plan's 200 Hz resampling, 60-second windows, 1-second FFT intervals, and fixed 179/22/34 patient split. The patient-wise evaluation uses three patient-disjoint folds, with test data for a class subsampled if it is much larger than training data for that class, while the seizure-wise evaluation uses five class-stratified folds with an 80:20 train-validation split. Training uses 100 epochs, batch size 64, cross-entropy loss, Adam optimizer, initial learning rate 0.01 with reduction to 1e-4, and early stopping after 10 epochs without validation-loss improvement. Primary metric is weighted F1-score, supplemented by sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV, AUC, and 95% confidence intervals; hardware and exact software versions are not reported in the provided excerpt.
