[method]
The paper evaluated seizure-type classification by converting 19-channel EEG into STFT spectrograms, vertically concatenating the channels into images, and using an overlap technique to balance training samples. It then compared two classification pipelines: transfer learning with pretrained CNNs (AlexNet, VGG16, VGG19, SqueezeNet, GoogLeNet, InceptionV3, DenseNet201, ResNet18, ResNet50, ResNet101) and feature extraction from pretrained network layers followed by an SVM with a radial basis kernel. The dataset was split 70% training and 30% testing, the experiment was repeated 10 times, and average classification accuracy was reported. This method is related to the proposed research method because both concern evaluation of seizure-type classification, but the paper uses overall accuracy rather than the proposed weighted F1-score, precision, and recall, so it does not directly quantify per-seizure-type performance or class imbalance effects.
[results]
Transfer learning achieved a highest accuracy of 82.85% with GoogLeNet using Adam and learning rate 1e-4. Other transfer-learning accuracies included 77.66%, 76.60%, and 74.47% for AlexNet with sgdm, rmsprop, and Adam; 67.02% for VGG16 and 65.96% for VGG19 with rmsprop at learning rate 1e-5; 74.74%, 78.72%, and 82.85% for GoogLeNet with sgdm, rmsprop, and Adam; 71.28%, 70.21%, and 68.21% for InceptionV3 with sgdm, rmsprop, and Adam at learning rate 1e-3; and 75.53% for DenseNet201 with Adam at learning rate 1e-4. For SqueezeNet transfer learning, the text stated that learning rate 1e-4 outperformed other learning rates across solvers, but did not provide the exact accuracy. Feature extraction with SVM achieved highest accuracies of 76.60% for AlexNet, 83.83% for VGG16, 81.82% for VGG19, 85.11% for SqueezeNet, 74.47% for GoogLeNet, 88.30% for InceptionV3, 85.11% for DenseNet201, 86.17% for ResNet18, 86.17% for ResNet50, and 87.23% for ResNet101. The overall best reported result was 88.30% using features from InceptionV3 layers 37, 41, and 58, compared with 82.85% for transfer learning. In the feature-extraction approach, accuracy generally improved in deeper layers, although DenseNet201 fluctuated in deeper layers, and ReLU layers showed the highest accuracy in most pretrained networks. The paper also reported high predicted probabilities for TN and TC and greater than 80% probabilities for AB, FN, GN, CP, and SP, but did not report weighted F1, precision, or recall.
[context]
The EEG signals were bandpass filtered at 0.1-44 Hz and transformed with STFT using a Kaiser window of length 63, shape parameter 1, 75% overlap, and FFT length 256. The 19-channel spectrograms were concatenated vertically to form images. Training samples were balanced using an overlap technique. The evaluation used a 70/30 train/test split and 10 repeated runs with average accuracy, without reporting cross-patient disjoint splits, hardware specifications, statistical significance, or per-class metrics. Dataset size and patient count were not specified in the provided sections. This differs from the research plan, which targets cross-patient evaluation on TUSZ with disjoint patient sets and proposes weighted F1-score, precision, and recall as the primary metrics for seizure-type performance.
