[method]
The paper's relevant method is an ablation and baseline evaluation of a DTCWT ScatterNet representation pipeline. For CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, 32x32 images were upsampled to 64x64 (R1) and 48x48 (R2), decomposed with DTCWT filters at 6 orientations and 5 or 4 scales, followed by L2 nonlinearity, optional parametric log transformation, smoothing, and concatenation of scattering coefficients at m=0,1,2. OLS selected discriminative dimensions, and a G-SVM classified the selected features. The paper compared accuracy across scales J=1-4, with and without log transformation, and with m=1 versus m=1,2 coefficients, and compared against Mallat's ScatterNet, unsupervised methods, and supervised deep models. This relates to the proposed ST-IRM ablation method because it examines how individual representation components and configuration choices affect performance, but it is an image-scattering ablation rather than an EEG ablation of patient groups, top-k graph neighbors, or penalty weights.
[results]
On CIFAR-10, OLS-selected features achieved 81.6% accuracy for R1 with log transformation versus 80.7% without, and 81.8% for R2 with log versus 80.9% without. Concatenating selected R1 and R2 dimensions achieved 82.4%. For R1, G-SVM accuracy on scale-specific coefficients increased from 62.7% at J=1 without log to 70.2% at J=4, and from 65.6% at J=1 with log to 72.4% at J=4; m=1 accuracy was 70.4% without log and 72.5% with log. Log transformation gave a small consistent improvement. On CIFAR-100, the pipeline achieved 56.7%. It outperformed Mallat's ScatterNet and unsupervised baselines, but underperformed supervised deep models by nearly 10%. In small-data CIFAR-10 experiments with fewer than 10,000 training images, it outperformed LeNet and Network in Network. Computationally, it extracted R1 and R2 coefficients in 0.78 s versus 0.98 s for Mallat's network on R1 only, and OLS selection took 2.21 h versus 3.22 h. The scattering vector was 44,796 dimensions versus 113,712 for Mallat's three-layer network, and OLS selected 2,300 dimensions versus 1,080, which the paper interprets as greater feature richness.
[context]
The experiments used CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, each with 50,000 training and 10,000 test 32x32 images. G-SVM parameters were c=14 and gamma=0.00002, selected by 5-fold cross-validation on training features. Log transformation parameters were k1=1.1, k2=3.8, k3=3.8, and k4=7 for scales 1-4. OLS selected 108 dimensions per class for R1, yielding 1,080 total dimensions, and 1,200 dimensions per image for R2; selecting more dimensions reduced accuracy. Small-data experiments divided the 50,000 training images into eight balanced subsets and used the full 10,000-image test set. Simulations were run on a server with 32 GB RAM per node under uniform conditions. This context is relevant to the research plan as an example of ablation, baseline comparison, and computational evaluation for invariant representation learning, but the paper does not use EEG, cross-patient splits, patient-group environments, top-k correlation graphs, or gradient variance penalties.
