[method]
The paper evaluates stochastic gradient descent with warm restarts, a learning-rate schedule for SGD with momentum that periodically resets the learning rate to an initial value and then anneals it toward zero within each cycle using cosine annealing. The schedule is controlled by an initial cycle length T0 and a multiplier Tmult, with tested settings including T0=50, 100, or 200 with Tmult=1, and T0=1 or 10 with Tmult=2. It is applied to Wide Residual Networks on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100, a motor-imagery EEG dataset, and a downsampled 32x32 ImageNet variant. This method is relevant to the proposed research method because the proposed plan tunes initial learning rate and training epochs on unseen-patient validation data, and the paper directly examines how initial learning rate, epoch budget, and cosine-annealing-style restart schedules affect final and anytime performance. It is also relevant to the broader research plan because the plan specifies a cosine annealing learning-rate scheduler for EEG-based seizure classification.
[results]
On CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 with WRN-28-10, default SGD with initial learning rate 0.1 gave median test error 4.24 and 20.33, while initial learning rate 0.05 gave 4.13 and 20.21. SGDR with T0=200 and Tmult=1 gave 3.86 and 19.98, and SGDR with T0=10 and Tmult=2 gave 4.03 and 19.58. A wider WRN-28-20 model with SGDR T0=10 and Tmult=2 reached 3.74 on CIFAR-10 and 18.70 on CIFAR-100. Snapshot ensembles from one SGDR run with three snapshots improved WRN-28-10 to 3.51 on CIFAR-10 and 17.75 on CIFAR-100; three runs with three snapshots each reached 3.25 and 16.64; and 16 runs with three snapshots each reached 3.14 and 16.21. On the EEG motor-imagery dataset, SGDR achieved similar final performance to the baseline schedule with better anytime performance, and snapshot aggregation improved the median reference error of about 9% by 1-2% when using snapshots from a single run and by 2-3% when using snapshots from two hyperparameter settings. On the downsampled ImageNet experiment, SGDR with T0=10, Tmult=2, and initial learning rate 0.01 achieved top-1 error 39.24% and top-5 error 17.17% after 40 epochs, comparable to AlexNet results on full-size ImageNet.
[context]
The CIFAR experiments used 50,000 training and 10,000 test 32x32 images, WRN-28-10 with 36.5M parameters or WRN-28-20 with 145.8M parameters, 200-epoch budgets, minibatch size 128, weight decay 0.0005, momentum 0.9, per-pixel mean subtraction, horizontal flips and random crops, and no ZCA whitening. The EEG experiment used 14 subjects with roughly 1000 trials per subject for right and left hand and foot movement classification, a CNN baseline, a 30-epoch baseline schedule with learning-rate drops by a factor of 10 at epochs 10, 15, and 20, and SGDR without a fixed total epoch budget. The downsampled ImageNet experiment used 32x32 images from 1000 classes, 40 preliminary epochs, and initial learning rates 0.050, 0.025, 0.01, and 0.005. Hardware details are not specified in the paper, but WRN-28-20 requires roughly three to four times more computation than WRN-28-10. These conditions differ from the proposed plan's cross-patient seizure classification on the TUSZ dataset, patient-disjoint validation, correlation-graph top-k selection, maximum diffusion steps, dropout probability, and unseen-patient validation tuning; the paper's relevance is therefore limited to learning-rate initialization, epoch budget, cosine-annealing-style schedules, and EEG-domain evidence rather than the full invariant spatiotemporal representation learning architecture.
[end related work 1]

