- in_text_citation: [(Asif et al., 2019-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/457931ded7106a4fa26bc84844e1323cecac231f)
- venue: MLCN/RNO-AI@MICCAI
- citation count: 94
[method]
The paper describes training an ensemble of deep CNNs, called SeizureNet, on saliency-encoded spectrograms derived from EEG signals. The model is trained with a cross-entropy loss applied to the combined logits of the ensemble, using softmax outputs and an indicator function for the true class. Network weights are initialized from zero-mean Gaussian distributions with a standard deviation of 0.01, and biases are initialized to 0. Training is implemented in Torch using the ADAM optimizer, a batch size of 50, 400 epochs, an initial learning rate of 0.001 reduced by a factor of 10 at 50% and 75% of training, and a parameter decay of 0.0005 on weights and biases. Evaluation is performed using seizure-level 5-fold cross-validation on TUH-EEGSC v1.4.0 and patient-level 3-fold cross-validation on TUH-EEGSC v1.5.2, with Myoclonic seizures excluded. This method relates to the proposed research method because it specifies training hyperparameters such as learning rate and training epochs, and it uses a patient-disjoint evaluation setting similar to the cross-patient validation goal in the research plan. However, the paper does not report explicit hyperparameter tuning on a separate unseen-patient validation set, nor does it include top-k neighbor selection, maximum diffusion steps, or dropout probability.
[results]
SeizureNet improved patient-wise weighted F1 scores by approximately 3 points and seizure-wise weighted F1 scores by approximately 4 points compared with existing methods. The paper attributes these improvements mainly to multi-spectral feature learning, which captures information from different frequency and spatial resolutions and enables more discriminative feature learning. The excerpt does not provide exact per-class F1 values, confidence intervals, or statistical significance tests.
[context]
Experiments used TUH-EEGSC v1.4.0, containing 2012 seizures, and TUH-EEGSC v1.5.2, containing 3050 seizures. Myoclonic seizures were excluded because only three examples were available, which was considered too low for meaningful analysis. For TUH-EEGSC v1.4.0, 5-fold seizure-wise cross-validation was used because the TN and SP seizure types contained data from only two patients, making patient-wise validation statistically weak. For TUH-EEGSC v1.5.2, 3-fold patient-wise cross-validation was used because the seven selected seizure types each contained data from at least three patients, and train and test seizures were always from different patients. The reported training configuration was fixed rather than tuned on a separate unseen-patient validation set, and hardware details are not provided in the excerpt.
