- venue: International Conference on Learning Representations
- citation count: 175
[method]
The paper used a patient-disjoint validation split to tune model configuration before final evaluation. The official TUSZ training set was randomly split by patients into 90 percent training and 10 percent validation, and the official TUSZ test set was held out after removing five patients who appeared in both the official training and test sets. The tuned models were graph neural networks, including distance-graph DCRNN and correlation-graph DCRNN, trained on log-amplitude FFT EEG clips, with Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines trained on the same preprocessed data. This is directly related to the proposed method because it optimizes model configuration on unseen-patient validation data, and the proposed hyperparameters such as learning rate, top-k correlation neighbors, diffusion steps, dropout, and epochs correspond to the training and graph-construction choices of the DCRNN family. The provided excerpt confirms that validation was used for hyperparameter tuning but does not list the exact selected values.
[results]
Using this validation-based tuning and patient-disjoint test evaluation, DCRNN without self-supervised pre-training performed on par with or better than baselines. With self-supervised pre-training, Dist-DCRNN achieved an AUROC of 0.875 for 60-second seizure detection, matching a CNN pretrained on a labeled dataset five times larger than TUSZ. For 12-second seizure detection at 25 percent false positive rate, pre-trained Dist-DCRNN achieved 84.3 percent true positive rate, pre-trained Corr-DCRNN achieved 81.7 percent, while Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM achieved 72.8 percent, 67.0 percent, and 62.5 percent, respectively. For 12-second seizure classification, non-pretrained Dist-DCRNN achieved 93 percent accuracy on rare absence seizures, 2 points above the best baseline, and pre-trained Dist-DCRNN achieved 74 percent accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, 47 points above Dense-CNN and 48 points above non-pretrained Dist-DCRNN. The excerpt reports AUROC, true positive rate, and class accuracy; it does not report final weighted F1 values or statistical significance tests for these tuned configurations.
[context]
