[method]
The paper evaluated stochastic gradient descent with warm restarts, a cosine-annealing-style learning-rate schedule that periodically resets the learning rate and can double the cycle length, on an EEG dataset of brain recordings for classifying right and left hand and foot movements. It compared a fixed-budget baseline learning-rate schedule with the warm-restart schedule and also tested ensembles built from model snapshots taken when the learning rate reached zero. This is related to the proposed method because the research plan uses a cosine annealing scheduler for training EEG clip models, although the paper does not implement the proposed invariant spatiotemporal representation learning, cross-patient seizure classification, or TUSZ dataset evaluation.
[results]
On the EEG dataset, the warm-restart schedule reached final performance similar to the best baseline schedule while providing better anytime performance without requiring the total number of epochs to be fixed in advance. Model snapshots improved the median reference error of about 9% by 1 to 2 percentage points when snapshots from a single run were ensembled, and by 2 to 3 percentage points when snapshots from two different hyperparameter settings were combined. No statistical significance tests or confidence intervals were reported for these EEG improvements.
[context]
The EEG experiment used a convolutional neural network pipeline from a prior EEG study, 14 subjects, roughly 1000 trials per subject, and a baseline 30-epoch schedule that dropped the learning rate by a factor of 10 at epochs 10, 15, and 20. The paper did not report batch size, hardware, or exact warm-restart cycle parameters for the EEG experiment, so it provides only indirect support for using cosine annealing and snapshot ensembles in the proposed cross-patient seizure-classification setup with 40-clip batches on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090.
