[metadata]
- in_text_citation: [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6)
- venue: International Conference on Learning Representations
- citation count: 175
[method]
The paper evaluates a graph neural network called DCRNN for EEG seizure detection and classification on TUSZ. It represents 19 standard 10-20 EEG channels as a graph, using either a distance graph or a correlation graph, and processes frequency-domain inputs obtained as log-amplitudes of the fast Fourier transform of raw EEG. For detection and self-supervised pre-training, it uses both seizure and non-seizure EEGs in non-overlapping 12-s and 60-s clips; for classification, it uses one 12-s or 60-s clip per seizure event. The seizure labels are reduced to four classes: combined focal, generalized non-specific, absence, and combined tonic. The model is trained with patient-wise splits: the official TUSZ train set is split 90/10 by patients into training and validation, and the official test set is held out after removing five patients appearing in both official train and test. The paper compares DCRNN with Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines and also reports self-supervised pre-training as an initialization for both detection and classification. This is related to the proposed research plan because the plan uses the same TUSZ v1.5.2 corpus, frequency-domain 60-s EEG clips, patient-disjoint evaluation, and lists Dist-DCRNN and Corr-DCRNN as baselines. The proposed implementation details of batch size 40, cosine annealing, and RTX 3090/Xeon Gold 6248R hardware are not specified in the paper excerpt, but they are the kind of training and computational setup used to train such GNN and self-supervised seizure models.
[results]
Without self-supervised pre-training, distance-graph DCRNN and correlation-graph DCRNN perform on par with or better than the Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines. With self-supervised pre-training, DCRNN improves on both detection and classification. Pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN reaches an AUROC of 0.875 for 60-s seizure detection, matching a CNN with AUROC 0.88 that was pre-trained on a labeled dataset five times larger than TUSZ. For 12-s detection at 25% false positive rate, pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN achieves 84.3% true positive rate, pre-trained correlation-graph DCRNN achieves 81.7%, while Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM achieve 72.8%, 67.0%, and 62.5%, respectively. For 12-s seizure classification, non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN achieves 93% accuracy on rare absence seizures, 2 points above the best baseline. Pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN achieves 74% accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, 47 points above Dense-CNN and 48 points above non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN. The paper also reports that many generalized non-specific seizures are misclassified as combined focal; manual review of 32 misclassified test cases found 27 were actually focal seizures mislabeled as generalized non-specific and 5 were truly generalized seizures misclassified by the model.
[context]
The experiments use TUSZ v1.5.2 with 5,612 EEGs and 3,050 annotated seizures, 19 EEG channels, and detailed seizure duration/location annotations for interpretability. The dataset is preprocessed into the frequency domain using FFT log-amplitudes, and clips are formed as non-overlapping 12-s or 60-s windows. The classification scheme excludes myoclonic seizures and merges focal non-specific, simple partial, and complex partial seizures into combined focal, and merges tonic-clonic with tonic seizures into combined tonic. Evaluation uses AUROC for seizure detection and weighted F1-score for seizure classification, with patient-disjoint train, validation, and test sets. The paper excerpt does not report batch size, learning-rate schedule, number of epochs, or hardware specifications; therefore the proposed batch size of 40 EEG clips, cosine annealing scheduler, and NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 with Intel Xeon Gold 6248R CPU are not directly validated by the paper but are relevant implementation conditions for reproducing or extending the TUSZ-based GNN and self-supervised baseline setup.
