[method]
The paper describes an empirical training and evaluation protocol for seizure detection and classification on EEG using a static distance-based graph over 19 standard 10-20 electrodes. Each EEG clip is resampled, segmented into non-overlapping windows, transformed by fast Fourier transform to log amplitudes of non-negative frequency components, and z-normalized per node and time point. The graph adjacency matrix is fixed by electrode placement geometry using a Gaussian kernel over inter-electrode distances. The proposed model, REST, uses two graph convolution layers for state updates, with the first layer using ReLU and the second using a linear activation, and is evaluated in deterministic, single random-mask, and multiple random-mask variants. Baselines include DCRNN, CNN-LSTM, LSTM, Transformer, GRU, and ResNet-LSTM. Detection uses clip-level seizure presence with MSE or binary cross-entropy, while classification uses cross-entropy and evaluates clips beginning 2 seconds before seizure onset over 10-second windows. This relates to the proposed method because the proposed ST-IRM plan also targets TUSZ-based seizure classification, uses comparable EEG preprocessing and graph/RNN baselines, and specifies a training and computational setup, although the proposed method uses batch size 40, cosine annealing, and RTX 3090 hardware rather than the paper's batch size 128, ADAM, and A100 hardware.
[results]
