[context]
Experimental conditions include the TUSZ dataset with 5545 EEG files, five seizure types, 19 channels, and resampling to 200 Hz, and the CHB-MIT dataset with 24 patients, 192 seizures, 19 channels, and 256 Hz. Frequency features contain 100 bins for TUSZ and 128 bins for CHB-MIT. Detection window lengths are 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, and 14 seconds for TUSZ and 4, 6, 8, 10, and 12 seconds for CHB-MIT. TUSZ training and validation are formed by a 90/10 split of the original training set, with a separate standardized evaluation set; CHB-MIT uses an 80/10/10 patient-disjoint train, evaluation, and test split. Training uses ADAM, a batch size of 128 EEG clips, five random seeds, and a single NVIDIA A100 GPU. The proposed method differs by using a batch size of 40 EEG clips, a cosine annealing learning-rate scheduler, and an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 with an Intel Xeon Gold 6248R CPU, so direct numerical transfer from the paper's setup to the proposed setup is not supported by the excerpt.
