  "support": "The strongest support comes from the newer TUSZ work [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6), which builds on the earlier patient-level cross-validation of [(Asif et al., 2019-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/457931ded7106a4fa26bc84844e1323cecac231f) and the earlier learning-rate-schedule evidence of [(Loshchilov, and Hutter, 2016-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b022f2a277a4bf5f42382e86e4380b96340b9e86) by explicitly using a patient-disjoint validation split on TUSZ v1.5.2 with 5,612 EEGs, 3,050 annotated seizures, 19 channels, log-amplitude FFT clips, and a 90 percent training and 10 percent validation patient split while holding out the official test set after removing five overlapping patients. This directly validates the proposed plan's core procedure of tuning model configuration on unseen-patient validation data before final cross-patient evaluation, and its distance-graph and correlation-graph DCRNN baselines are the same model family for which the plan tunes top-k correlation neighbors, maximum diffusion steps, dropout probability, initial learning rate, and training epochs. The reported outcomes, including pre-trained Dist-DCRNN AUROC 0.875 for 60-second seizure detection, 84.3 percent TPR at 25 percent FPR for 12-second detection versus Dense-CNN 72.8 percent, LSTM 67.0 percent, and CNN-LSTM 62.5 percent, and large rare-class accuracy gains, show that patient-disjoint validation plus graph GNNs can yield clinically meaningful cross-patient performance. The next strongest support is [(Asif et al., 2019-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/457931ded7106a4fa26bc84844e1323cecac231f), which evaluated SeizureNet on TUH-EEGSC v1.4.0 with 2,012 seizures and v1.5.2 with 3,050 seizures using patient-level 3-fold cross-validation for v1.5.2, matching the plan's cross-patient evaluation goal and weighted F1 metric. Its fixed training recipe, Adam with batch size 50, 400 epochs, initial learning rate 0.001 with factor-10 reductions at 50 percent and 75 percent of training, and weight decay 0.0005, provides a concrete seizure-domain baseline for the plan's initial learning rate and epoch budget, and its reported approximately 3-point patient-wise and 4-point seizure-wise weighted F1 improvements demonstrate that patient-disjoint evaluation can capture meaningful gains. Supporting evidence for the learning-rate and epoch components also comes from [(Loshchilov, and Hutter, 2016-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b022f2a277a4bf5f42382e86e4380b96340b9e86), which tested SGDR with cosine-annealing restarts on CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 WRN-28-10 and WRN-28-20, a motor-imagery EEG dataset, and downsampled ImageNet. It shows that initial learning rate and epoch budget materially affect performance: default SGD with initial learning rate 0.1 gave median test error 4.24 on CIFAR-10 and 20.33 on CIFAR-100, while SGDR T0=200 and Tmult=1 gave 3.86 and 19.98, and T0=10 and Tmult=2 gave 4.03 and 19.58; on EEG, SGDR matched final performance with better anytime performance and snapshot aggregation improved median reference error by 1 to 2 percent from one run and 2 to 3 percent from two hyperparameter settings. This supports tuning initial learning rate and epochs and using a cosine-annealing-style schedule, though it does not validate the plan's exact ST-IRM architecture. The weakest support is [(Haderlein et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/57c88580ceb478d93bc0e33146c25576f4eea1f0), which used validation-based model selection for intracranial EEG seizure prediction, but only with within-patient temporal splits, linear regularized classifiers, 10-fold cross-validation, and no reported quantitative outcomes; it supports the general principle of validation-based tuning but provides little direct evidence for unseen-patient tuning of deep graph hyperparameters.",
  "contradictions": "The strongest quantitative contradiction is that [(Asif et al., 2019-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/457931ded7106a4fa26bc84844e1323cecac231f) achieved patient-level weighted F1 improvements on TUH-EEGSC v1.5.2 with a fixed hyperparameter configuration, Adam with batch size 50, 400 epochs, initial learning rate 0.001 with factor-10 reductions at 50 percent and 75 percent of training, and weight decay 0.0005, without reporting tuning on a separate unseen-patient validation set, which weakens the assumption that validation-based tuning of initial learning rate and epochs is necessary for cross-patient seizure classification. A second contradiction is that [(Loshchilov, and Hutter, 2016-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b022f2a277a4bf5f42382e86e4380b96340b9e86) shows that tuning only initial learning rate and epochs with a cosine-annealing-style schedule may be insufficient: SGDR with restarts improved CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 median test error from 4.24 and 20.33 for default SGD to 3.86 and 19.98 for T0=200 and Tmult=1, and snapshot ensembles further improved performance, whereas the plan lists only cosine annealing and does not tune T0, Tmult, or snapshot aggregation. A third limitation is that the closest TUSZ work, [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6), confirms patient-disjoint validation tuning but does not disclose the selected top-k, diffusion-step, dropout, learning-rate, or epoch values, and its largest gains are tied to self-supervised pre-training, for example pre-trained Dist-DCRNN 84.3 percent TPR at 25 percent FPR, so the plan's specific hyperparameter list is not independently validated. The weakest contradiction is [(Haderlein et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/57c88580ceb478d93bc0e33146c25576f4eea1f0), which used validation-based tuning but with within-patient temporal splits and linear regularized classifiers on 15 intracranial EEG patients, with no reported quantitative outcomes; this suggests unseen-patient validation may not be the only appropriate strategy for small patient cohorts, but the absence of results makes it weak evidence.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important action is to make the validation protocol explicit and robust: before final evaluation, remove any patients appearing in both training and test sets as in [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6), and because the plan uses only 22 validation patients, use repeated patient-disjoint splits or nested cross-validation to select initial learning rate, top-k correlation neighbors, maximum diffusion steps, dropout probability, and training epochs, reporting mean and variance of weighted F1, precision, and recall across splits. Second, expand the optimization search beyond initial learning rate and epochs to include the full learning-rate schedule, comparing cosine annealing with SGDR-style restarts using T0 and Tmult and with step-decay schedules such as the 0.001 initial learning rate with factor-10 reductions at 50 percent and 75 percent used by [(Asif et al., 2019-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/457931ded7106a4fa26bc84844e1323cecac231f), while also evaluating snapshot ensembles or model averaging as suggested by [(Loshchilov, and Hutter, 2016-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b022f2a277a4bf5f42382e86e4380b96340b9e86), since that work showed snapshot aggregation improved EEG median reference error by 1 to 2 percent from one run and 2 to 3 percent from two settings. Third, explicitly ablate the graph-specific hyperparameters on the validation set, including top-k correlation neighbors, maximum diffusion steps, dropout, and correlation versus distance graph construction, using the DCRNN and MSTGCN baselines in the plan and reporting the selected values, because [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6) demonstrates the relevance of correlation-graph DCRNNs but does not disclose the tuned values. Fourth, incorporate self-supervised pre-training or invariant SSL as a separate ablation, since pre-trained Dist-DCRNN achieved 84.3 percent TPR at 25 percent FPR for 12-second detection and 0.875 AUROC for 60-second detection, and compare 60-second clips with 12-second clips to match the plan's 60-second segmentation while assessing sensitivity to clip length. Fifth, report per-class weighted F1, precision, recall, AUROC, and TPR at a fixed FPR, with confidence intervals and statistical significance tests, and handle rare seizure types transparently, following [(Asif et al., 2019-03)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/457931ded7106a4fa26bc84844e1323cecac231f), which excluded Myoclonic seizures due to only three examples. Finally, specify the computational budget and early-stopping rule for the RTX 3090 experiments, including batch size 40, maximum epochs, and whether validation-based early stopping will be used, and consider a temporal or leave-patient-out sensitivity analysis inspired by [(Haderlein et al., 2023-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/57c88580ceb478d93bc0e33146c25576f4eea1f0) if patient-specific variability dominates the cross-patient signal.",
