  "support": "The strongest support for the proposed training and computational setup comes from [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6), which uses the same TUSZ v1.5.2 corpus, 19 standard 10-20 channels, FFT log-amplitude frequency features, non-overlapping 60-second clips, patient-disjoint train/validation/test splits, weighted F1 for classification, and the same baseline family listed in the plan, including Dense-CNN, LSTM, CNN-LSTM, Dist-DCRNN, and Corr-DCRNN. Its self-supervised pretraining improves detection and classification, with pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN reaching AUROC 0.875 for 60-second seizure detection, 84.3 percent true positive rate at 25 percent false positive rate for 12-second detection versus 72.8, 67.0, and 62.5 percent for Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM, and 74 percent accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, 47 points above Dense-CNN and 48 points above non-pretrained DCRNN. This directly supports the plan's TUSZ preprocessing, cross-patient evaluation, baseline choices, and the value of a self-supervised component, although it does not report batch size, learning-rate schedule, or hardware. The next strongest support for the specific cosine annealing scheduler is [(Loshchilov, and Hutter, 2016-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b022f2a277a4bf5f42382e86e4380b96340b9e86), which evaluated stochastic gradient descent with warm restarts on an EEG CNN with 14 subjects and roughly 1000 trials per subject, using a 30-epoch baseline that dropped the learning rate by a factor of 10 at epochs 10, 15, and 20. The warm-restart schedule achieved final performance similar to the best fixed-budget baseline while improving anytime performance, and snapshot ensembling improved median reference error by 1 to 2 percentage points from one run and by 2 to 3 percentage points from two hyperparameter settings, providing indirect EEG-specific support for cosine-style annealing and snapshot ensembles, but not for TUSZ, cross-patient seizure classification, batch size 40, or RTX 3090. A weaker but conceptually relevant support is [(Grill et al., 2020-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/38f93092ece8eee9771e61c1edaf11b1293cae1b), whose BYOL self-supervised method uses an online encoder-predictor, an exponential moving average target encoder, projection and predictor MLPs, and no negative examples; the supplied excerpt reports that a fixed randomly initialized target yields 18.8 percent top-1 linear ImageNet accuracy versus 1.4 percent for the random network alone, supporting the use of a non-collapsing self-supervised target mechanism in the plan's SSL component, but it is image-based and does not validate EEG clip batches, cosine annealing, or the proposed hardware. Additional weaker support comes from [(Afzal et al., 2024-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6dcfaacb4333afb240719af712366a059ccc67a3), which uses TUSZ with 5545 EEG files, 19 channels, 200 Hz resampling, FFT log amplitudes, z-normalization, and graph/RNN baselines, showing that such TUSZ graph seizure models are trainable with ADAM, batch size 128, five seeds, and a single A100, but it provides no numerical accuracy or F1 in the excerpt and therefore only weakly supports the proposed setup. The weakest support is [(Gijsen, and Ritter, 2024-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/915f5a6b0970b37f4dca99ddcdade054b2e6a96f), which applies a residual CNN EEG encoder and MLP projection heads to TUSZ with 5-second crops, 5-fold cross-validation, subject exclusion from pretraining, and temperature tau=0.3, but the excerpt contains no quantitative results, so it supports leakage-aware evaluation more than the proposed 60-second clip training setup.",
  "contradictions": "The most important contradiction is not a reported failure of batch size 40, cosine annealing, or RTX 3090, but the absence of direct validation for that exact setup in the closest TUSZ training protocol. [(Afzal et al., 2024-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6dcfaacb4333afb240719af712366a059ccc67a3) trains comparable TUSZ graph seizure models with ADAM, batch size 128 EEG clips, five random seeds, and a single NVIDIA A100, whereas the proposed method uses batch size 40, cosine annealing, and an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 with Intel Xeon Gold 6248R CPU; this mismatch means the proposed setup may be less stable, slower, or memory-constrained, and the excerpt also reports that MSE outperformed binary cross-entropy for seizure detection, which could contradict a binary detection loss if the plan ever uses BCE for clip-level seizure presence. A second tension is that [(Gijsen, and Ritter, 2024-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/915f5a6b0970b37f4dca99ddcdade054b2e6a96f) evaluates TUSZ seizure/background classification with 5-second crops and 5-fold cross-validation on train and dev sets, with subject exclusion from pretraining, rather than the plan's non-overlapping 60-second clips and fixed patient-disjoint 179/22/34 split; this does not prove the proposed split is wrong, but it shows that shorter crops and cross-validation are also used, and the excerpt provides no quantitative outcomes to support the proposed 60-second setup. A third tension is that [(Grill et al., 2020-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/38f93092ece8eee9771e61c1edaf11b1293cae1b) validates BYOL on ImageNet with ResNet-50, 224x224 crops, 2048-dimensional pooled features, and 4096-to-256 projection/predictor MLPs, but does not specify batch size, learning-rate schedule, or hardware, so it cannot support direct transfer of the proposed 40-clip EEG batch, cosine annealing, or RTX 3090/Xeon Gold 6248R setup. A fourth tension is that [(Loshchilov, and Hutter, 2016-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b022f2a277a4bf5f42382e86e4380b96340b9e86) does not report batch size, hardware, exact warm-restart cycle parameters, or statistical significance for its EEG experiment, so its support for cosine annealing is indirect and insufficient to justify the proposed scheduler without additional ablation. Finally, [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6) is the closest TUSZ evidence but also omits batch size, learning-rate schedule, epochs, and hardware, so it supports the scientific pipeline but not the specific computational choices.",
  "suggested_action": "The most important action is to justify the proposed batch size, scheduler, and hardware with a TUSZ-specific pilot ablation. Following [(Afzal et al., 2024-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6dcfaacb4333afb240719af712366a059ccc67a3) and [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6), train Dist-DCRNN, Corr-DCRNN, Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM on the same 19-channel, 200 Hz, FFT log-amplitude, 60-second TUSZ v1.5.2 clips with patient-disjoint splits, then compare batch size 40 versus 128, cosine annealing versus step decay or constant learning rate, and RTX 3090 versus A100 if available, using at least five random seeds and reporting weighted F1, precision, recall, AUROC where applicable, variance, memory, and wall-clock time. If RTX 3090 memory limits batch size 128, use gradient accumulation to reach an effective batch size of 128 or explicitly report why batch size 40 is chosen. The next most important action is to specify the cosine annealing schedule using the EEG evidence from [(Loshchilov, and Hutter, 2016-08)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b022f2a277a4bf5f42382e86e4380b96340b9e86), including T_max, warm restarts, final learning rate, number of epochs, and whether snapshots at zero learning rate are ensembled; because that paper reported 1 to 2 percentage point improvements from single-run snapshots and 2 to 3 percentage point improvements from two hyperparameter settings without significance tests, the plan should add confidence intervals and significance tests for snapshot ensembling. Then, for the self-supervised component, adapt the BYOL design from [(Grill et al., 2020-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/38f93092ece8eee9771e61c1edaf11b1293cae1b) to EEG frequency features by using an EMA target encoder, projection and predictor MLPs, and a collapse check, and include an ablation with a fixed randomly initialized target, since BYOL's fixed target still achieved 18.8 percent top-1 linear ImageNet accuracy versus 1.4 percent for the random network alone. Also incorporate the loss and preprocessing cautions from [(Afzal et al., 2024-06)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6dcfaacb4333afb240719af712366a059ccc67a3): use z-normalized FFT log amplitudes, a static distance graph with Gaussian kernel if graph baselines are used, cross-entropy for seizure-type classification, and MSE rather than binary cross-entropy if any binary seizure/background detection objective is included. Finally, address leakage and evaluation consistency using [(Gijsen, and Ritter, 2024-09)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/915f5a6b0970b37f4dca99ddcdade054b2e6a96f) by excluding TUSZ/TUEV subjects from any external pretraining, keeping the primary evaluation patient-disjoint, and optionally adding a 5-second crop ablation to test whether shorter crops improve rare seizure-type classification, while retaining the 60-second non-overlapping clips supported by [(Tang et al., 2021-04)](https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0a4d5fdfaba62390b25c725badffee524bbcf0a6) as the primary setup.",
