when one or more Transformer blocks were fine-tuned.', 'context': "Experiments used ImageNet-1K for self-supervised pretraining and evaluation with a single 224x224 validation crop. The main comparison used ViT-L/16, with high-ratio random patch masking, an asymmetric encoder-decoder design, and fine-tuning for 50 epochs versus 200 epochs for supervised training from scratch. For the stronger reported models, pretraining was run for 1600 epochs; ViT-L training on 128 TPU-v3 cores took 31 hours for 1600 epochs, compared with 36 hours for 300 epochs for MoCo v3. Downstream COCO evaluation adapted the ViT backbone to Mask R-CNN with FPN. The paper's setting is natural-image classification and transfer, not cross-patient EEG seizure classification, and it does not evaluate distribution shift or invariant risk minimization."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3445, 'output_tokens': 4576}, {'corpus_id': '9778889', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '5799324', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1922, 'output_tokens': 303}, {'corpus_id': '211677999', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates Risk Extrapolation (REx) for domain generalization, with two variants: MM-REx and V-REx. MM-REx minimizes average training risk plus a robust term over affine combinations of domain risks with coefficients bounded by a hyperparameter lambda_min; negative lambda_min extrapolates beyond the convex hull of domain risks and, as lambda_min approaches negative infinity, enforces equality of domain risks, while lambda_min=0 recovers risk interpolation. V-REx minimizes average risk plus beta times the variance of domain risks, where beta=0 recovers empirical risk minimization and large beta enforces equal risks. The paper also argues that exact risk equalization can support causal discovery under assumptions that the causes of the label are observed. The experimental setting described in detail is Colored MNIST, a binary classification task in which digits 0-4 and 5-9 are collapsed into two classes, color is a spurious feature strongly correlated with the label in training, and the color-label correlation is reversed at test time. The model must learn digit shape and ignore color. The paper compares V-REx, MM-REx, IRM, risk interpolation, empirical risk minimization, and a grayscale oracle using test accuracy. The paper also evaluates on DomainBed and reinforcement learning tasks, but the provided excerpt gives detailed quantitative results for Colored MNIST.', 'results': 'On Colored MNIST, V-REx achieved 71.5 ± 1.0 training accuracy and 68.7 ± 0.9 test accuracy. IRM achieved 70.8 ± 0.9 training accuracy and 66.9 ± 2.5 test accuracy. MM-REx achieved 72.4 ± 1.8 training accuracy and 66.1 ± 1.5 test accuracy. Risk interpolation achieved 88.9 ± 0.3 training accuracy and 22.3 ± 4.6 test accuracy. Empirical risk minimization achieved 87.4 ± 0.2 training accuracy and 17.1 ± 0.6 test accuracy. A grayscale oracle achieved 73.5 ± 0.2 training accuracy and 73.0 ± 0.4 test accuracy, with an optimum of 75 and chance of 50. V-REx outperformed IRM by 1.8 test accuracy points and MM-REx by 2.6 test accuracy points, but remained 4.3 points below the grayscale oracle. The paper also reports that REx outperforms IRM on a wide range of out-of-distribution tasks, but when inherent noise in the label varies across environments, IRM succeeds and REx performs poorly.', 'context': "The Colored MNIST experiment uses three training domains, a binary digit classification task, and a test distribution in which the spurious color-label association is reversed; evaluation is by accuracy. The provided excerpt does not specify hardware, optimizer, batch size, or exact hyperparameter values for these experiments. This paper is relevant to the proposed ST-IRM research plan because both use multiple environments or domains to encourage invariance: the proposed method partitions patients into groups and applies invariant risk minimization with a gradient variance penalty, while the paper uses V-REx to penalize variance of domain risks and MM-REx to robustly extrapolate across domain risks. The paper's evidence that equalizing risks can suppress spurious features supports using patient groups as environments to remove patient-specific EEG variation, but its warning that REx can fail when label noise varies across environments is directly relevant to EEG, where artifacts, noise, and patient-specific electrophysiological variation may differ across patient groups. The proposed mask and self-supervised temporal loss are not present in the paper, but the paper's risk-equality principle provides a related inductive bias for separating invariant seizure-related signals from variant patient-specific signals."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3401, 'output_tokens': 5183}, {'corpus_id': '3631386', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '27040966', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2014, 'output_tokens': 1012}, {'corpus_id': '227407962', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1764, 'output_tokens': 327}, {'corpus_id': '17957759', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '238861687', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1824, 'output_tokens': 733}, {'corpus_id': '232352665', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1691, 'output_tokens': 225}, {'corpus_id': '265930176', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1858, 'output_tokens': 3549}, {'corpus_id': '272259486', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1763, 'output_tokens': 706}, {'corpus_id': '233296355', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper evaluates a graph neural network called DCRNN for EEG seizure detection and classification, using distance-based and correlation-based graphs over 19 EEG channels. Raw EEG is transformed into log-amplitude fast Fourier transform frequency-domain inputs, then segmented into non-overlapping 12-second or 60-second clips for detection and self-supervised pre-training, and into one clip per seizure event for classification. The model is trained in a patient-disjoint setup on the TUSZ v1.5.2 dataset, using a 90/10 patient-wise split of the official training set for training and validation, while the official test set is held out after removing five patients who appear in both the official train and test sets. The component most relevant to the proposed ST-IRM method is the self-supervised pre-training of DCRNN, where pre-trained weights are used for both seizure detection and seizure classification. This relates to the proposed method's self-supervised learning component and its goal of improving cross-patient generalization, although the paper does not use invariant risk minimization, mask-based invariant/variant decomposition, patient-environment clustering, or gradient variance penalties.", 'results': 'Without self-supervised pre-training, distance-graph DCRNN and correlation-graph DCRNN perform on par with or better than Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines on seizure detection and classification, and outperform reported CNN results on seizure classification. With self-supervised pre-training, DCRNN improves on both tasks. For 60-second seizure detection, pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN reaches an AUROC of 0.875, matching the performance of a supervised CNN pre-trained on a labeled dataset five times larger than TUSZ. For 12-second seizure detection at a 25% false positive rate, pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN achieves 84.3% true positive rate and pre-trained correlation-graph DCRNN achieves 81.7%, compared with 72.8% for Dense-CNN, 67.0% for LSTM, and 62.5% for CNN-LSTM. For 12-second seizure classification, non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN achieves 93% accuracy on rare absence seizures, which is 2 points above the best baseline. Pre-trained distance-graph DCRNN achieves 74% accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, which is 47 points above Dense-CNN and 48 points above non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN. A neurologist review of 32 misclassified generalized non-specific test seizures found that 27 were likely focal seizures and only 5 were true generalized seizures, suggesting that label noise contributes to apparent misclassification.', 'context': 'Experiments use TUSZ v1.5.2 with 5,612 EEGs, 3,050 annotated seizures, eight original seizure types, and 19 standard 10-20 channels. For classification, the paper uses a refined four-class scheme: combined focal, generalized non-specific, absence, and combined tonic. Myoclonic seizures are excluded because only three examples are available, and tonic-clonic seizures are merged with tonic seizures because tonic-clonic seizures begin with a tonic phase and the tonic and tonic-clonic classes are rare. Detection and self-supervised pre-training use both seizure and non-seizure EEGs, while classification uses only seizure EEGs with one clip per seizure event. Evaluation metrics are AUROC for detection and weighted F1-score for classification, with additional ROC curves and confusion matrices. The provided text does not specify hardware, exact optimizer, learning rate, batch size, or full hyperparameter values, but the experimental conditions include patient-disjoint splits, frequency-domain log-FFT inputs, 12-second and 60-second clip lengths, and comparison against Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines trained on the same preprocessed data.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3910, 'output_tokens': 3286}, {'corpus_id': '231719568', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper describes an EEG pretraining and downstream fine-tuning pipeline implemented with the DN3 library and BENDR models. To reduce hardware- and dataset-induced distribution shifts, each EEG sequence was linearly scaled and shifted so that its maximum and minimum values became 1 and -1, respectively, and an additional constant channel equal to the sequence's original max-min range was appended to retain relative amplitude information. The network was trained with Adam using weight decay 0.01 and a cosine learning-rate decay schedule with linear warm-up for 5% of pretraining steps and 10% of fine-tuning steps; peak learning rate and other hyperparameters were dataset-dependent. This is related to the proposed ST-IRM plan because both aim to improve EEG generalization under distribution shift, but the paper's method addresses shift mainly through normalization, pretraining, and fine-tuning rather than through an invariant mask, patient-environment partitioning, or invariant risk minimization.", 'results': 'No quantitative experimental outcomes are provided in the supplied paper excerpt. The Results section is empty, so no classification accuracy, F1-score, baseline comparison, ablation result, or statistical significance can be reported for the preprocessing or pretraining/fine-tuning method.', 'context': 'The experimental context is limited to the methods excerpt: experiments used the DN3 library, pretrained BENDR models were made available, and the preprocessing goal was to make downstream EEG datasets resemble the pretraining dataset configuration. No dataset names, patient counts, seizure types, electrode counts, hardware specifications, batch sizes, or evaluation metrics are specified in the excerpt. The only reported training conditions are Adam with weight decay 0.01, cosine decay with linear warm-up of 5% for pretraining and 10% for fine-tuning, and dataset-dependent peak learning rates.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2112, 'output_tokens': 2163}, {'corpus_id': '211096730', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper studies a contrastive self-supervised representation-learning framework for visual data. For each input image, two correlated views are generated by sampling stochastic augmentations, including random crop and resize, color distortion, and Gaussian blur. A ResNet encoder maps each view to a representation, and a one-hidden-layer MLP projection head with a ReLU nonlinearity maps that representation to the space where the contrastive loss is computed. The loss is a normalized temperature-scaled cross-entropy objective over all 2N augmented examples in a minibatch, where the other augmented examples in the batch serve as negatives. After training, the projection head is discarded and the encoder is evaluated with linear probing and fine-tuning. This method is relevant to the proposed ST-IRM framework because ST-IRM also uses a self-supervised loss to shape representations before supervised seizure-type prediction; the paper’s design choices are directly analogous to deciding how the self-supervised loss should operate on invariant EEG representations across time steps, whether a separate projection head should be used, and how augmentation or masking should remove spurious patient-specific cues while preserving seizure-relevant structure.', 'results': 'In the augmentation ablation, no single transformation alone produced strong linear-evaluation performance, even though the model could identify positive pairs in the contrastive task. Composing random crop with color distortion produced the strongest tested pair scores, with the two crop-color entries in the reported ablation matrix at about 55.8 and 56.3 linear-evaluation score, while many other single transformations or pairs were lower, with several below 40. Stronger color augmentation improved linear evaluation of the unsupervised models, but the same stronger color augmentation did not improve and could hurt supervised models trained with the same augmentations. A sophisticated supervised augmentation policy did not outperform simple cropping plus stronger color distortion. The paper also reports that a nonlinear projection head improves the quality of the preceding encoder representation, and that larger batch sizes and longer training benefit contrastive learning. These results are relevant to ST-IRM because they show that self-supervised objectives learn useful representations only when the auxiliary task is made sufficiently hard by removing spurious shortcuts; for cross-patient EEG, this supports careful ablation of the mask function, patient-group environments, and time-step self-supervision to ensure the model preserves invariant seizure-type signals rather than patient-specific artifacts. The provided excerpt does not report statistical significance values for these comparisons.', 'context': 'The experiments were conducted on visual image data using ResNet encoders. Unsupervised contrastive models were trained for 100 or 1000 epochs and compared with supervised ResNet baselines trained for 90 epochs. Evaluation used linear probing and fine-tuning of the learned encoder. The augmentation study used an asymmetric setting in which one branch received crop and resize plus the tested transformation while the other branch was left as the identity transformation, in order to isolate the effect of individual augmentations and their compositions. Exact batch sizes, temperature values, and hardware specifications are not specified in the provided excerpt. The domain is natural images, not EEG, so the findings are methodological evidence for self-supervised representation learning rather than direct validation of cross-patient seizure classification on the TUSZ dataset.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4503, 'output_tokens': 4203}, {'corpus_id': '245218767', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3632, 'output_tokens': 3036}, {'corpus_id': '232075606', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1738, 'output_tokens': 1933}, {'corpus_id': '7862216', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '260351051', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '47252984', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '10215400', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '251589354', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1736, 'output_tokens': 432}, {'corpus_id': '69955714', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '257174184', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '218971783', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 8200, 'output_tokens': 488}, {'corpus_id': '211069320', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a self-supervised signal-transformation recognition network to learn ECG representations for downstream emotion recognition. ECG signals from four public datasets are downsampled to 256 Hz, high-pass filtered at 0.8 Hz, normalized with user-specific z-score normalization, and segmented into non-overlapping 10-second windows. Each segment is transformed using six pretext operations: noise addition, scaling, permutation, time-warping, temporal inversion, and negation. A multi-task convolutional network trained in TensorFlow predicts which transformation was applied, with the total loss computed as a weighted average of the six transformation-classification losses. The learned encoder is then used for emotion recognition. This relates to the proposed spatiotemporal invariant risk minimization plan because it is a self-supervised representation-learning strategy for physiological time series and examines whether multi-task and multi-dataset self-supervision improves downstream generalization, but it does not use invariant risk minimization, patient-level environments, or an explicit invariant/variant mask.', 'results': 'The provided excerpt does not report numerical accuracy, F1-score, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance. It reports qualitatively that multi-task self-supervision consistently outperforms single-task self-supervision, with stronger gains on AMIGOS and DREAMER. It also reports that training the self-supervised network on all four datasets combined improves emotion recognition compared with training on individual datasets, with significant improvements for AMIGOS, DREAMER, and WESAD and only marginal improvement for SWELL. The identified optimal parameter vector is SNR 15, scaling factor 0.9, 20 permutation segments, 9 time-warping segments, and stretch factor 1.05. The loss weights are 0.0125 for temporal inversion and negation, and 0.195 for the other five transformation tasks.', 'context': 'Experiments use four public ECG emotion datasets with different hardware, sampling rates, and collection protocols. Preprocessing is intentionally minimal: 256 Hz sampling, 0.8 Hz high-pass IIR filtering, user-specific z-score normalization, and 10-second non-overlapping windows. Evaluation uses 10-fold cross-validation with 90 percent training and 10 percent testing after shuffling, while parameter analyses use a single 90-10 split. Training uses the Adam optimizer with learning rate 0.001 and batch size 128; the transformation recognition network is trained for 100 epochs and the emotion recognition network for 250 epochs. Hardware specifications and subject counts are not provided in the excerpt. The setting is ECG-based emotion recognition, not cross-patient EEG seizure classification, and the evaluation is not patient-disjoint, so it does not directly match the proposed cross-patient invariant learning setup.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5004, 'output_tokens': 3222}, {'corpus_id': '44061081', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1977, 'output_tokens': 1781}, {'corpus_id': '236950460', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1725, 'output_tokens': 723}, {'corpus_id': '62841734', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5008, 'output_tokens': 2071}, {'corpus_id': '227118869', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper uses SimSiam, a self-supervised siamese representation-learning method. It takes two augmented views of an image, processes both with a shared encoder consisting of a ResNet-50 backbone and a three-layer projection MLP, and uses a two-layer prediction MLP on one branch to predict the other branch's normalized encoder output. The loss is the symmetrized negative cosine similarity between the predicted vector and the stop-gradient-applied target vector, equivalent to mean squared error on L2-normalized vectors up to a scale factor. Stop-gradient is applied to the target in each term so that one branch's encoder does not receive gradient from its own target in that term. This is relevant to the proposed ST-IRM plan because the plan also uses a self-supervised loss to align invariant representations across time steps; SimSiam provides a collapse-avoiding self-supervised alignment objective that could be adapted to EEG time-step or augmented-view pairs, although the paper does not include invariant masking, environment partitioning, invariant risk minimization, or cross-patient EEG evaluation.", 'results': 'On ImageNet linear evaluation, SimSiam was trained with batch size 256 and used neither negative samples nor a momentum encoder. The excerpt reports that it achieved competitive results, had the highest accuracy among compared methods under 100-epoch pre-training, and outperformed SimCLR in all cases, though its gain from longer training was smaller. Exact SimSiam accuracy values are not included in the provided text. In transfer-learning experiments on VOC and COCO object detection and instance segmentation, SimSiam representations were competitive with leading methods after 200-epoch ImageNet pre-training; a recipe with learning rate 0.5 and weight decay 1e-5 produced better results in all tasks than the base recipe. Related ablations reported values of 66.5, 66.4, and 66.0 for SimCLR with predictor and predictor plus stop-gradient, and 66.5 for SwAV with predictor, 65.2 and NaN when stop-gradient was removed, indicating that stop-gradient behavior is method-specific.', 'context': 'Experiments used ImageNet with standard ResNet-50 and two 224x224 augmented views during pre-training. Default pre-training used SGD with base learning rate 0.05 scaled by batch size divided by 256, cosine decay, weight decay 0.0001, momentum 0.9, batch size 512 by default, and synchronized batch normalization; the reported SimSiam ImageNet comparison used batch size 256. The projection MLP had three layers, hidden dimension 2048, batch normalization on each fully connected layer including output, and no ReLU on output. The prediction MLP had two layers, input/output dimension 2048, hidden dimension 512, batch normalization on hidden layers, and no batch normalization or ReLU on output. Ablation pre-training used 100 epochs, while transfer-learning comparisons used 200-epoch pre-training and end-to-end fine-tuning on target datasets. The setup was described as friendly to typical 8-GPU implementations, but specific hardware for the main results is not detailed in the excerpt.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3855, 'output_tokens': 3790}]): Request timed out.
Error processing ('Context: To preserve temporal consistency of invariant EEG representations. Method: We will implement a self-supervised learning approach that focuses on preserving relationships between invariant representations across time steps, using the loss function: Lssl = (1/|nT|) Σᵢ₌₁ⁿ Σₜ₌₁ᵀ L(zt-1(m(φ(Xᵢₜ₋₁))), m(φ(Xᵢₜ))).', [{'corpus_id': '243985980', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper implements a masked autoencoder for self-supervised visual representation learning. Images are divided into non-overlapping patches, a high fraction of patches is randomly sampled without replacement and removed, and the encoder processes only the visible patches without mask tokens. A lightweight decoder then reconstructs the full pixel image from the encoder latent representation and mask tokens. The method is evaluated by pretraining on ImageNet-1K and then assessing representations through linear probing, end-to-end fine-tuning of Vision Transformer backbones, and transfer learning to object detection and segmentation with Mask R-CNN and FPN. This is related to the proposed EEG method in that both use masking and self-supervised pretraining to learn representations before supervised classification, but the paper masks image patches for pixel reconstruction rather than applying a learned mask to EEG features to isolate invariant components, and it does not include a temporal consistency loss, patient grouping, or invariant risk minimization across patient environments.', 'results': 'For ViT-L/16 on ImageNet-1K, supervised training from scratch achieved 76.5 percent top-1 accuracy in the original recipe and 82.5 percent with the paper stronger regularization recipe, while MAE pretraining followed by 50-epoch fine-tuning reached 84.9 percent. Larger models improved further: ViT-H reached 86.9 percent at 224x224 and 87.8 percent at 448x448, exceeding the reported previous best of 87.1 percent at 512x512 using only ImageNet-1K. In COCO transfer, MAE pretraining improved Mask R-CNN box AP over supervised pretraining by 2.4 points for ViT-B, from 47.9 to 50.3, and by 4.0 points for ViT-L, from 49.3 to 53.3. The method was also reported to be faster than token-based BEiT by about 3.5 times per epoch and to require less total pretraining time than some contrastive baselines when compared on the same hardware. The provided excerpt does not report confidence intervals or statistical significance tests.', 'context': 'Experiments used ImageNet-1K training data with no external data, single 224x224 crop validation, random patch masking with a high masking ratio, no data augmentation, and an asymmetric encoder-decoder design. The main ablation used ViT-L/16, and stronger models used ViT-H. Pretraining for the main comparisons used 1600 epochs; on 128 TPU-v3 cores, ViT-L MAE pretraining took 31 hours for 1600 epochs, compared with 36 hours for 300 epochs for a contrastive baseline. Fine-tuning used 50 epochs rather than 200 epochs from scratch. Transfer learning used Mask R-CNN with FPN on COCO and reported box AP and mask AP. These conditions are relevant to the proposed EEG research plan as a benchmark for self-supervised pretraining followed by supervised fine-tuning, but they do not include EEG signals, temporal sequences, cross-patient disjoint splits, seizure-type labels, or invariant risk minimization across patient environments.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3497, 'output_tokens': 3645}, {'corpus_id': '20639205', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1695, 'output_tokens': 743}, {'corpus_id': '233296355', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper evaluates a self-supervised pre-training stage for a graph-based DCRNN on TUSZ EEGs. Raw 19-channel EEGs are converted to log-amplitude FFT features and segmented into non-overlapping 12-s or 60-s clips. A distance-graph or correlation-graph DCRNN is first pretrained in a self-supervised manner, described in the appendix as using self-supervised prediction as an auxiliary task, and then fine-tuned with supervised labels for seizure detection and classification. This is related to the proposed Lssl because both use temporal self-supervision to shape EEG representations before supervised seizure-type prediction; however, the paper's method does not include the proposed invariant mask m(·), orthogonal invariant/variant decomposition, patient-environment IRM, or gradient variance penalty, so it is a partial analogue rather than the full ST-IRM plan.", 'results': 'Self-supervised pre-training improved DCRNN performance on both tasks. For 60-s seizure detection, pre-trained Dist-DCRNN reached AUROC 0.875, matching a CNN pretrained with supervised labels on a dataset five times larger than TUSZ. For 12-s detection at 25% false positive rate, pre-trained Dist-DCRNN achieved 84.3% true positive rate and pre-trained Corr-DCRNN 81.7%, compared with 72.8% for Dense-CNN, 67.0% for LSTM, and 62.5% for CNN-LSTM. For 12-s classification, non-pretrained Dist-DCRNN achieved 93% accuracy on rare absence seizures, 2 points above the best baseline; pre-trained Dist-DCRNN achieved 74% accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, 47 points above Dense-CNN and 48 points above non-pretrained Dist-DCRNN. The paper also reports that DCRNN outperforms two previously reported CNNs on seizure classification, but exact weighted F1 values are not included in the provided excerpt.', 'context': 'Experiments used TUSZ v1.5.2 with 5,612 EEGs, 3,050 annotated seizures, and eight seizure types; five patients present in both official train and test sets were removed to ensure patient-disjoint evaluation. The official train set was split 90/10 by patient into training and validation, and the official test set was held out. Inputs were frequency-domain log-amplitude FFT features from 19 standard 10-20 channels, with 12-s and 60-s non-overlapping clips; classification used one clip per seizure event. The classification scheme was reduced to four classes: combined focal, generalized non-specific, absence, and combined tonic, because some focal types are not EEG-distinguishable and rare classes were merged. Evaluation used AUROC for detection and weighted F1 for classification. The provided excerpt does not report hardware, optimizer, learning rate, or the exact self-supervised loss, so reproducibility of the pre-training objective is limited; the proposed plan adds explicit invariant masking, temporal consistency loss, patient clustering, and IRM on top of this type of temporal self-supervised DCRNN setup.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3962, 'output_tokens': 3441}, {'corpus_id': '272259486', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1815, 'output_tokens': 633}, {'corpus_id': '13957365', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '231719568', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper describes a pretraining and downstream fine-tuning pipeline for EEG deep neural networks implemented with the DN3 library. The extracted text does not specify the pretraining objective, dataset names, or evaluation metrics, but it does specify that EEG sequences from multiple datasets were preprocessed to create a consistent input representation. Each EEG sequence was linearly scaled and shifted so that its maximum and minimum values became 1 and -1, respectively. To preserve relative amplitude information lost by this normalization, one additional channel was appended with the constant per-sequence value max(s_i) - min(s_i). Training used the Adam optimizer with weight decay 0.01 and a cosine learning-rate decay schedule with linear warm-up for 5% of pretraining steps and 10% of fine-tuning steps. The peak learning rate and other hyperparameters were dataset-dependent and deferred to an appendix. This method is related to the proposed research method only at the level of learning EEG representations for downstream tasks; the paper does not describe an invariant mask, a temporal self-supervised loss, or a cross-patient seizure classification setup.', 'results': 'No quantitative outcomes, baseline comparisons, or statistical significance values are reported in the provided RESULTS section; the extracted results section is empty.', 'context': 'The available experimental context is limited to the extracted methods text: EEG sequences from multiple datasets with hardware differences, per-sequence amplitude normalization to [-1, 1], addition of one constant channel encoding the per-sequence amplitude range, Adam optimization with weight decay 0.01, cosine learning-rate decay with 5% warm-up for pretraining and 10% warm-up for fine-tuning, dataset-dependent peak learning rates, and implementation using the DN3 library. Dataset identities, sample sizes, hardware specifications, evaluation metrics, and cross-patient evaluation details are not specified in the provided text.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2164, 'output_tokens': 3635}, {'corpus_id': '211096730', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper implements SimCLR, a contrastive self-supervised representation learning framework for images. For each image, two correlated views are generated by sampling augmentations from a family; the main pipeline sequentially applies random crop and resize to 224x224, random color distortion, and random Gaussian blur. A ResNet base encoder produces h, a one-hidden-layer MLP projection head with ReLU produces z, and NT-Xent loss is minimized using all other 2(N-1) augmented examples in the minibatch as negatives, with cosine similarity and temperature tau. After training, the projection head is discarded and the encoder is evaluated by linear probing and fine-tuning. This relates to the proposed EEG method because both use a self-supervised alignment objective to train an encoder before supervised classification; the proposed loss aligns invariant masked representations m(phi(X_it-1)) and m(phi(X_it)) across adjacent 1-second EEG intervals, analogous to aligning two views, but the paper's views are augmented image crops/color/blur rather than temporal EEG intervals and it does not use an invariant mask, patient environments, or invariant risk minimization.", 'results': 'In the asymmetric augmentation ablation, linear evaluation scores showed that no single transformation was sufficient: crop alone scored 33.1, cutout 25.6, color distortion 18.8, Sobel 4.0, Gaussian noise 9.8, Gaussian blur 2.6, and rotation 2.6. Pairwise compositions improved scores, with crop plus color distortion highest at 56.3 when crop was the first transformation and 55.8 when color distortion was first. Other crop pairs were crop plus Sobel 46.0/46.2, crop plus Gaussian noise 39.9/38.8, crop plus Gaussian blur 35.0/35.1, crop plus cutout 33.9/32.2, and crop plus rotation 30.2/30.0. The paper reports that stronger color augmentation substantially improved linear evaluation of unsupervised models, while AutoAugment did not outperform simple crop plus stronger color distortion. When the same augmentations were used for supervised ResNets, stronger color augmentation did not improve and could hurt performance. Statistical significance values were not provided in the supplied sections.', 'context': "The experiments were conducted on ImageNet-style images, with random crop and resize to 224x224 as the base spatial transformation. The augmentation ablation used an asymmetric setting: one branch received the crop/resize plus targeted transformation while the other branch was identity, which the authors note hurts performance but isolates augmentation effects. Evaluation used linear probing of the learned encoder, with unsupervised models trained for 100 or 1000 epochs and supervised ResNet baselines trained for 90 epochs. The provided sections do not specify exact batch size, temperature, optimizer, hardware, or statistical tests. For the EEG research plan, this context indicates that the paper's evidence is limited to static image classification and augmentation design, not cross-patient EEG, temporal consistency, seizure labels, or invariant risk minimization; it can inform the need to ablate the proposed temporal self-supervised loss and invariant mask rather than assume that any self-supervised alignment will yield invariant cross-patient representations."}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 4555, 'output_tokens': 4459}, {'corpus_id': '245218767', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a self-supervised masked feature prediction method for video and image representation learning. For video, an MViT encoder is trained on unlabeled video clips by randomly masking 40% of space-time cubes and predicting target features at the masked positions. The target features include hand-designed or learned representations such as HOG, dVAE, and pseudo-labels. The video experiments use random resized cropping and horizontal flipping, 224x224 resolution, and cube-wise masking. This is related to the proposed EEG method because both approaches use self-supervised learning to learn spatiotemporal representations that should remain useful across time, but the paper learns by predicting masked space-time features rather than directly aligning invariant masked representations between adjacent time steps or enforcing cross-patient invariance.', 'results': 'On Kinetics-400, 300 epochs of self-supervised pretraining improved the scratch MViT-S 16x4 model by 1.1 points to 81.1% top-1 accuracy. The paper also reports using 800 epochs of pretraining for larger MViT-L 16x4 models, but the provided excerpt does not include the corresponding top-1 or top-5 values. For ImageNet-1K, the paper reports that the method significantly improves scratch ViT-B and ViT-L baselines and is on par with or outperforms supervised pretraining on a larger labeled image dataset for ViT-L at 384x384 resolution, but exact top-1 values are not included in the provided text. Statistical significance is not reported in the excerpt.', 'context': 'The video experiments used improved MViT base and large models, pretraining only on unlabeled Kinetics-400 training clips, 224x224 resolution, 40% space-time cube masking, minimal augmentation, and downstream fine-tuning. Pretraining lasted 300 epochs for MViT-S and 800 epochs for MViT-L. The image experiments used vanilla ViT-B and ViT-L, 224x224 pretraining on unlabeled ImageNet-1K training data, 40% block-wise masking, minimal augmentation, 1600 pretraining epochs, and 100 or 50 fine-tuning epochs for ViT-B and ViT-L respectively. Hardware details are not specified in the provided excerpt. These conditions are relevant to the proposed EEG research plan because they show that self-supervised spatiotemporal pretraining can improve downstream classification, but the paper does not evaluate cross-patient EEG, invariant risk minimization, or a mask-based invariant/variant decomposition.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3684, 'output_tokens': 3191}, {'corpus_id': '268041792', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '260351051', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '47252984', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '251589354', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1788, 'output_tokens': 1365}, {'corpus_id': '211069320', 'analysis': {'method': "The paper's closest related method is a multi-task self-supervised signal transformation recognition network for ECG emotion recognition. It learns ECG representations by training a CNN to distinguish original 10-second ECG segments from six generated transformations: noise addition, scaling, permutation, temporal inversion, negation, and time-warping. The temporal transformations, especially permutation, temporal inversion, and time-warping, are related to the proposed method because they encourage the model to capture temporal structure and invariances in physiological time series, similar to the proposed temporal consistency loss between invariant representations at adjacent time steps. The multi-task self-supervision and multi-dataset training are also related to the research plan's goal of learning robust spatiotemporal representations across heterogeneous subjects, although the paper does not use an invariant mask, invariant risk minimization, gradient variance penalty, or cross-patient EEG seizure classification.", 'results': 'The paper reports that multi-task self-supervised representation learning consistently outperformed single-task self-supervision, with more prominent gains on AMIGOS and DREAMER. The optimal multi-task parameter vector was SNR 15, scaling factor 0.9, 20 permutation segments, 9 time-warping segments, and stretch factor 1.05. Single-task analysis showed that emotion recognition F1 was best when pretext tasks had moderate difficulty; performance dropped when transformations were too easy, such as SNR greater than 35, scaling factor between 0.95 and 1.05, or 2 permutation segments, and also when transformations were too distorted, such as SNR less than 10, more than 20 permutation segments, scaling factor less than 0.6 or greater than 1.4, or stretch factor greater than 1.4. Training all four datasets together improved emotion recognition over training on individual datasets, with significant improvements for AMIGOS, DREAMER, and WESAD and marginal improvement for SWELL. Exact F1 values are not provided in the supplied text.', 'context': 'Experiments used four ECG datasets: AMIGOS, DREAMER, WESAD, and SWELL. Signals were downsampled to 256 Hz where needed, high-pass filtered at 0.8 Hz, user-specific z-score normalized, and segmented into non-overlapping 10-second windows. Evaluation used 10-fold cross-validation with 90 percent training and 10 percent testing repeated 10 times after shuffling; parameter analyses used a single 90-10 split. The transformation recognition network was trained for 100 epochs and the emotion recognition network for 250 epochs using Adam with learning rate 0.001 and batch size 128 in TensorFlow. Loss weights were 0.0125 for temporal inversion and negation and 0.195 for the other five transformation losses. Temporal inversion and negation losses saturated after 5 to 7 epochs, which motivated their smaller loss weights. The paper evaluates emotion recognition F1 accuracy, not cross-patient seizure classification metrics, and does not report hardware details in the provided excerpt.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5056, 'output_tokens': 3641}, {'corpus_id': '227407962', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1816, 'output_tokens': 438}, {'corpus_id': '62841734', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper summarizes self-supervised video feature learning methods that use temporal pretext tasks, including temporal order verification and temporal context prediction. In this approach, a video encoder is trained on unlabeled video data to solve a task generated from temporal structure, such as recognizing the correct order of video segments or exploiting temporal context, and the learned encoder is then fine-tuned for downstream action recognition. This relates to the proposed EEG method because both use a self-supervised objective over sequential observations to encourage representations that are stable or predictable across time steps; however, the paper’s method is visual, does not decompose invariant and variant components, and does not use patient-specific environments or invariant risk minimization.', 'results': 'The reported outcome is aggregate rather than for a single named temporal pretext method: the best self-supervised video feature learning methods achieved less than 66% accuracy after fine-tuning on UCF101, while a supervised model trained with Kinetics labels exceeded 84% accuracy. The paper states that self-supervised video feature learning remains much lower than supervised pretraining and that more effective methods are needed. No statistical significance, per-method accuracy, or exact comparison to a temporal-order baseline is provided in the excerpt.', 'context': 'The experimental context is survey-level: various video datasets and different base network architectures are used for self-supervised pretraining, and evaluation is conducted by fine-tuning on commonly used human action recognition benchmarks, UCF101 and HMDB51. The excerpt does not specify hardware, hyperparameters, exact dataset splits, or the precise loss for temporal order verification. This context is relevant to the EEG research plan as evidence that self-supervised temporal objectives can be used before supervised classification, but it does not address cross-patient distribution shift, invariant EEG representations, or seizure-type classification.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 5060, 'output_tokens': 3834}, {'corpus_id': '257174184', 'analysis': {'method': 'N/A', 'result': 'No paper text available for this reference.', 'context': 'N/A'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 0, 'output_tokens': 0}, {'corpus_id': '227118869', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper evaluates SimSiam, a non-contrastive self-supervised learning method that processes two augmented views of an image with a shared encoder f and a prediction MLP h. The encoder f consists of a ResNet-50 backbone plus a 3-layer projection MLP with batch normalization on each fully connected layer, hidden dimension 2048, and an output layer without ReLU. The prediction MLP h has 2 layers, input and output dimension 2048, hidden dimension 512, batch normalization on the hidden layer, and an output layer without batch normalization or ReLU. The loss is a symmetrized negative cosine similarity between the predicted representation of one view and the stop-gradient target representation of the other view, equivalent to mean squared error between L2-normalized vectors up to a scale of 2. This method is related to the proposed EEG research method because the proposed Lssl also aligns a transformed previous-time invariant representation, z_{t-1}(m(phi(X_{i,t-1}))), with a current-time invariant representation, m(phi(X_{i,t})). SimSiam provides a relevant template for using a predictor and stop-gradient to preserve relationships between two representations without negative samples or a momentum encoder, which could be adapted from image augmentations to adjacent EEG time steps and invariant mask outputs.', 'results': 'On ImageNet linear evaluation with a standard ResNet-50 and two 224x224 views, SimSiam was trained with batch size 256, no negative samples, and no momentum encoder. The paper reports that SimSiam achieves competitive results, has the highest accuracy among compared methods under 100-epoch pre-training, and outperforms SimCLR in all reported cases, although its gain from longer training is smaller. The provided excerpt does not state the exact SimSiam ImageNet linear evaluation accuracy. In transfer learning to VOC and COCO object detection and instance segmentation after 200-epoch ImageNet pre-training, SimSiam is competitive with leading methods, and a recipe with learning rate 0.5 and weight decay 1e-5 gives better transfer results than the base recipe while having similar ImageNet accuracy. Specific ablation values reported for related methods are: SimCLR with predictor 66.5, SimCLR with predictor and stop-gradient 66.4 and 66.0; SwAV with predictor 66.5, SwAV with stop-gradient removed 65.2 and then NaN. These outcomes are relevant to the proposed research plan because they show that a predictor and stop-gradient can support self-supervised representation learning, but the necessity of stop-gradient depends on the objective: it was not helpful for SimCLR with negatives, while removing it from SwAV caused degradation and divergence. This informs the design of the proposed temporal invariant Lssl, especially if it is used as a non-contrastive objective for EEG invariant representations.', 'context': 'Experimental conditions include ImageNet pre-training with SGD, base learning rate 0.05 scaled by batch size divided by 256, cosine decay, weight decay 0.0001, momentum 0.9, default batch size 512 for ablations but batch size 256 for the main ImageNet comparison, synchronized batch normalization, 100-epoch pre-training for ablations, and 200-epoch pre-training for transfer comparisons. The method is described as friendly to typical 8-GPU implementations. Evaluation uses ImageNet linear probing, kNN validation accuracy as a progress monitor, and end-to-end fine-tuning on VOC and COCO detection and instance segmentation with per-method fine-tuning learning-rate search. This context matters for the proposed EEG study because the paper demonstrates that a simple self-supervised alignment objective can be trained without large-batch LARS or negative sampling, but the image-domain results do not directly validate temporal EEG invariance or cross-patient seizure classification. For the proposed TUSZ-based plan, analogous controls would be needed for temporal sequence length, invariant mask dimensionality, batch size of 40 EEG clips, collapse monitoring, and whether stop-gradient is applied to the current-time invariant target in Lssl.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3907, 'output_tokens': 4552}, {'corpus_id': '243937121', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 1783, 'output_tokens': 1117}, {'corpus_id': '247446906', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper uses a graph-based DCRNN for EEG seizure detection and classification on the TUSZ dataset. Raw EEG signals from 19 standard 10-20 channels are transformed into log-amplitude fast Fourier transform frequency-domain inputs. The EEG channels are represented as either a distance graph or a correlation graph, and a DCRNN processes the graph-structured EEG clips. A self-supervised pre-training stage is applied to seizure and non-seizure 12-second and 60-second clips, after which the pretrained DCRNN weights are used to initialize supervised models for downstream seizure detection and classification. This method is related to the proposed research method because both use self-supervised learning on sequential TUSZ EEG clips to improve seizure classification, but the paper’s approach is a clip-level self-supervised pre-training baseline rather than an explicit step-level loss that preserves invariant representations across time steps. It also does not use an invariant mask, patient-environment partitioning, invariant risk minimization, or a gradient variance penalty as described in the proposed plan.', 'results': 'Without self-supervised pre-training, distance-graph DCRNN and correlation-graph DCRNN performed on par with or better than Dense-CNN, LSTM, and CNN-LSTM baselines. With self-supervised pre-training, distance-graph DCRNN achieved an AUROC of 0.875 for 60-second seizure detection, comparable to a CNN pretrained with supervised labels on a dataset about five times larger. At a 25% false positive rate on 12-second clips, pretrained distance-graph DCRNN reached 84.3% true positive rate and pretrained correlation-graph DCRNN reached 81.7%, compared with 72.8% for Dense-CNN, 67.0% for LSTM, and 62.5% for CNN-LSTM. For 12-second seizure classification, non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN achieved 93% accuracy on rare absence seizures, 2 points above the best baseline. Pretrained distance-graph DCRNN achieved 74% accuracy on rare combined tonic seizures, 47 points above Dense-CNN and 48 points above non-pretrained distance-graph DCRNN. The same pretrained weights were used for both detection and classification, indicating task-general initialization. The paper also reports that frequency-domain inputs substantially outperformed time-domain inputs, although exact values are not provided in the included sections. A neurologist reviewed 32 misclassified generalized non-specific test seizures and found that 27 were likely focal seizures, suggesting label noise contributed to apparent misclassification. No statistical significance tests are reported.', 'context': 'Experiments used TUSZ v1.5.2, containing 5,612 EEGs and 3,050 annotated seizures across eight seizure types. The authors used 19 EEG channels, log-amplitude FFT preprocessing, and non-overlapping 12-second or 60-second clips for seizure detection and self-supervised pre-training. For seizure classification, one 12-second or 60-second clip was taken from each seizure event. The seizure taxonomy was reduced to four classes: combined focal, generalized non-specific, absence, and combined tonic. Data were split by patient, with a 90/10 train/validation split from the official training set and the official test set held out after removing five patients who appeared in both the official train and test sets. Evaluation used AUROC for seizure detection and weighted F1-score for seizure classification. Baselines were trained and evaluated on the same preprocessed data. Hardware, the exact self-supervised objective, and full hyperparameters are not specified in the provided sections. Relative to the proposed research plan, this paper provides a relevant self-supervised pre-training baseline on the same TUSZ frequency-domain EEG setup, but it focuses on general graph GNN initialization rather than explicit invariant spatiotemporal representation learning across patient environments.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 3962, 'output_tokens': 4743}, {'corpus_id': '7961699', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2820, 'output_tokens': 1487}, {'corpus_id': '253370631', 'analysis': {'method': 'The paper used a reconstruction-based self-supervised pretraining approach for EEG, described as BENDR/MAEEG, which is related to the proposed method because both use masked EEG features and self-supervised objectives to learn temporally structured representations for downstream classification. Raw 6-channel 100 Hz EEG was passed through six convolutional layers with dropout, GroupNorm, and GELU to produce 64-dimensional temporal features at approximately 1.05 Hz. A mask selected a subset of feature tokens and replaced them with Gaussian noise N(0, 1/64). The masked sequence was encoded by an 8-layer transformer encoder with convolutional relative positional embeddings to 192-dimensional features, then mapped by a final convolutional layer to 64-dimensional contextual features. A contrastive loss compared contextual features with the corresponding unmasked features. The paper then varied mask rate and number of mask chunks to study how masking affects learned representations and downstream sleep-stage classification. Unlike the proposed invariant spatiotemporal framework, this method did not decompose features into invariant and variant components, did not use a learned mask m(·) in [0,1], did not apply invariant risk minimization, and did not target cross-patient seizure-type classification.', 'results': 'The paper reported that higher masking percentages with fewer chunks, producing longer masked spans, increased the SSL pretraining loss, indicating a harder reconstruction/contrastive task. Downstream performance differences were largest when only 0.1% of downstream labels were available, corresponding to about 4 hours of labeled data. Under this low-label condition, masking 75% of tokens performed better overall than masking 50% or 25%, and a single mask chunk outperformed multiple chunks. All MAEEG-pretrained models outperformed models without pretraining. The same trend—longer single mask spans performing better than shorter spans under limited labels—was reported for both MAEEG and BENDR. The excerpt did not provide exact classification scores, confidence intervals, or statistical significance values, so the outcomes are relative comparisons rather than quantitative absolute performance.', 'context': 'Experiments used the 2018 PhysioNet Challenge sleep EEG dataset, containing overnight polysomnography from 994 subjects, about 7,000 hours, 65% male, mean age 55 years ± 14.4, split into 596 training, 198 validation, and 200 test subjects. Each polysomnogram included 6-channel EEG, ECG, and respiratory signals, with labels for five sleep stages (wake, N1, N2, N3, REM) and respiratory-effort-related arousals assigned in 30-second non-overlapping windows. The paper focused on 6-channel EEG for five-stage sleep classification. Models were implemented in PyTorch 1.8.1; hardware specifications were not provided. This context is relevant to the proposed research plan because it demonstrates that masked self-supervised pretraining on EEG can improve low-label downstream classification, but it differs from the proposed plan in task (sleep stages rather than cross-patient seizure types), channel count (6 rather than 19), temporal segmentation (30-second windows rather than 60-second clips segmented into 1-second intervals), and absence of invariant/variant masking, environment partitioning, and gradient variance penalties.'}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 2661, 'output_tokens': 3275}, {'corpus_id': '218971783', 'analysis': {}, 'cost': 0, 'input_tokens': 8252, 'output_tokens': 753}]): Request timed out.
